window.harnessChunks["rows-3a6df061f6e7393b.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:32:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentForeman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kumizeod/AgentForeman/tree/634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 634f9c0906f133fc577d6291c29b8f8cfd6af02a\">634f9c0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce candidate code/experiments and general task artifacts through model decisions with returned evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous goals, failed code/training, poor candidates and contradictory evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose experiment/repair/retest/stop and instructions; author source or artifacts; revise after independent criticism. - Decision owner: configured model-backed manager, coding worker, contributors and integrator, invoked by first-party protocols. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: source snapshots, typed responses, worker pool leases, edit allowlist, native training/checks, archives and round/deadline bounds. - Closure path: verified baseline → manager model choice → worker action → independent measurement/criticism → next choice or revision → preserved terminal result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model roles leaves deterministic transport and checks but no substantive hypothesis, edit or report decision. - Caveats: A is structural ownership, not proof of live model quality; external coding tools are credited only as explicitly assembled task actors. - Boundary reachability: CLI campaign/overnight and cooperate/debate commands assemble these roles and execute their chosen actions; they are shipped supported modes, not test-only prompts. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/engine.py; src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/poolworker.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately closed inter-S1 interference regulation at the declared task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent contributions can be inconsistent; worker contention and duplicate execution are mechanically bounded. - Decisive decision or feedback right: integrator chooses combined output, but contributors are not subsequently coordinated through a specific inter-unit correction loop. - Decision owner: integrator owns S1 synthesis; runtime owns fixed sequencing/leases, with no separately evidenced autonomous S2 discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: independent assignments, integration contract, queue/lease locks, quota cooldown and critique of combined artifacts. - Closure path: contributors return once → integrator revises the combined product; duplicate jobs are suppressed by fixed IDs/locks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: decomposition, waiting for all contributions and rewriting one combined answer do not establish mutual adjustment of operational units. - Caveats: does not deny coordination in a broader organization assembled around this tool. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/cooperate.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py; docs/COOPERATIVE.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system discretionary current-control function over a portfolio established at this boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate failure, incumbent comparison, quota waits and bounded task duration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager chooses the next experiment within one fixed objective; it cannot change resources, task goal or budgets. - Decision owner: model owns local S1 research choices; operator/configuration selects task scope, role pool and limits; runtime enforces those choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: campaign rounds/best record, budget/deadline checks, role registry, UI status, cancel and resume. - Closure path: measured rounds return to a next-task-action choice; generic stop/confirmation returns no evidenced whole-system resource bargain. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the campaign manager name does not turn its local experimental policy into a metasystem; fixed quotas are enforcement. - Caveats: broader multi-project allocation may be performed externally but is outside the declared first-party campaign mode. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/pool.py. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge operational claims through separate model audit/critique with complementary original evidence and corrective return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported synthesis claims, missed criticism, inadequate experiment plans and false candidate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: distinct critic/auditor chooses anchored findings and accept/revise/reject; campaign reviewer can block or return an executable proposal for revision. - Decision owner: separately invoked model reviewer/auditor roles; deterministic gates apply verdict consistency and run trusted checks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: distinct role identities, read-only review snapshot, source/check/artifact anchors, issue ledger, frozen verifier and bounded revision/retest. - Closure path: raw artifacts/source/check receipts → separate critic judgment → integrator/manager revision and recheck, or stopped/needs-evidence/rejected result; council audit conditions final disposition. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model audit judgment owns substantive criticism; deterministic parsing and trusted checkpoint measurement are support, not the owner of that judgment. - Caveats: distinct identities are not statistically independent models; supplied sources are not authenticated by quote matching, and live factual correctness remains unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: documented campaign --review, cooperative distinct-reviewer mode and council audit are shipped runtime modes; no developer fixture is needed to select them. - Claim being audited: candidate/proposal or chair/integrator output is supported by original source material, controls, actual artifacts and check results. - Ordinary reporting path: manager rationale, proposer artifacts and chair/integrator summary. - Complementary access path: reviewer receives copied source snapshots plus experiment evidence; general critics receive full artifact/source/check anchor documents, and council auditor sees original answers and all criticism rather than chair summary alone. - Independence boundary: separate read-only sessions/role identities from author/integrator/chair; no authorship permission for the reviewer. Trusted verifier directly reads tensors without importing candidate code, adding corroboration rather than autonomous judgment. - Who acts on findings: campaign manager/integrator revises under recorded issue IDs; controller blocks/returns or marks needsevidence/rejected instead of fabricating success. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/discussion.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/templates/moons/verify.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external-and-prospective adaptation loop changing AgentForeman operational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new research hypotheses, failed experiment results, provider quota changes and user task uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: manager may change candidate experiment code, not harness capability/organization; task chair clarifies current requirements. - Decision owner: task agents own research outcomes; maintainer/operator owns external product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: research plans, measurements, council sources, quota information and roadmap. - Closure path: research findings return to the current task; no prospective environmental judgment returns as a change to harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research as the primary operational transformation is S1; internal optimization and quota reaction do not establish S4. - Caveats: an operator could use outputs to redesign its organization, but that external act is not a shipped adaptation closure. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/manager.py; src/agentforeman/campaign.py; src/agentforeman/council.py; src/agentforeman/briefing.py; docs/ROADMAP.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop established beyond bounded task contracts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unclear scope, changed deliverables/acceptance/autonomy and stale user confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human confirms a task contract; agents must pause when they report a scope change, rather than decide ultimate policy. - Decision owner: operator owns the local task mandate; product identity remains maintainer-defined outside runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: version/fingerprint confirmation, allowed-decision/pause fields, frozen checks, scope-change signal and stop controls. - Closure path: briefing → exact human confirmation → bound child task; requested scope changes pause for new clarification. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: this is operational task admission and authority bounding; it is not an identity/ultimate-policy dispute between present operations and future adaptation. - Caveats: contract editing is a real returned human decision but does not by itself satisfy the function-first S5 threshold. - Evidence: src/agentforeman/briefing.py; src/agentforeman/taskcontract.py; src/agentforeman/debate.py; docs/BRIEFING.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801/assessments/ag/agentforeman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8100f851f4f20a52d8b11424bbccf95b1d296801\">8100f85 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:13:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness² CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/3ae3ae/harness2-cli/tree/0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 0c811344a4edfe34960b2fc8f286369d7e81fb27\">0c81134 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Bounded model-backed improvement work per encountered task: examine operational evidence, edit a harness, probe the edited candidate and produce a selected task harness plus result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user problems and actual coding-agent failures demand different instruction, skill, script or hook changes; candidate edits can also violate a harness component contract. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Configured improver chooses candidate file changes and explains them against evidence. The model selector (in automatic mode) chooses a final task result where multiple candidates exist. - Decision owner: Model-backed proposer/editor and optional automatic selector, invoked through the first-party session interface. External executor cognition is a dependency, but the improvement goal and agent-edit control path are shipped by the reviewed CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Workspace harvest, component allowlist, tree validation, per-stage retry/fallback, rollout adapter, result/trajectory records and candidate selection parser. - Closure path: Current base-task rollout → independent agent-edited harness trees → validated candidate task probes → composed candidate and selector result → accepted task-harness and evidence for later reuse. - Boundary reachability: cli.run constructs Proposer with the installed channel config and invokes schedule.runsweep; runtask calls recursion.runmode, whose runstage actually invokes Proposer.call with writable context/harness and captures its changed tree, then probes candidates. All are included in the shipped CLI mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes contentful harness edits and, when configured, evidence-driven task-result choices; source-local Python merely bounds and enforces those decisions. Dry-run, static tests and deterministic fixtures by themselves do not prove model autonomy. - Evidence: run.py, recursion.py, proposer.py, schedule.py. - Basis: Supported live-model session path at the frozen commit, not asserted empirical success rate. - Confidence: High for source-wired decision ownership; outcome quality untested. - Caveats: The target coding assistant and provider are externally configured and not extra first-party viable S1 units.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No evidenced S2-specific stabilization among two distinct, separately viable owned S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent candidate and domain execution might consume resources, but distinct S1 divisions and an inter-unit oscillation/collision control relation are not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic concurrency width, domain ordering, semaphore and run-label isolation coordinate experimental steps, not an agentic inter-S1 conflict response. - Decision owner: Static Python scheduler and operator configuration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ThreadPoolExecutor, proposer semaphore, namespace isolation, mode/domain libraries and run budget. - Closure path: Scheduled experiments complete and aggregate; no S2-specific sensed interference and returned reciprocal adjustment across autonomous operating units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Stage-level parallelism, composition and result pooling are parts of one bounded optimization process, not S2 because they run concurrently. - Evidence: schedule.py, recursion.py, proposer.py. - Basis: Scoped source negative after examining task and domain concurrency. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: An external multi-agent product using this CLI might provide S2 independently.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-current control relationship over independently viable operational divisions at this recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Budget overruns, candidate failures, task completion, user approval and project file drift are local run/deployment constraints rather than multi-unit resource/commitment governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The operator sets execution budget, task set and allowed surface; runtime enforces limits; selectors compare results. None revises current commitments across distinct S1 units with systemic authority. - Decision owner: CLI user for configuration, deterministic scheduler for enforcement and model for candidate choice; no separately supported S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Budget/call caps, run status, concurrency limit, retries, apply conflict checks and --force option. - Closure path: Fixed run settings → bounded task experimentation → output; absent whole-current S3 view, discretionary priority/resource intervention and returned binding adjustment to multiple ongoing units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Picking a better harness is an operational or prospective adaptation choice; enforcing an operator&#39;s preset budget is not agent-owned S3 and ordinary apply approval is not parent-governed whole-system control. - Evidence: run.py, schedule.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative of whole-organization current-control closure. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Operator may manage a larger organization, but its current control is not implemented by this application.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independently complementary audit channel challenges routine S1–S3 reports and closes findings into corrective systemic control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Bad candidate harness files, inaccurate agent result claims and poor task answers are checked by local tree validation, rollout comparisons, benchmark judging or optional evaluation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Ordinary candidate validators, task selector and eval compare outputs; none has separately independent authority to audit a standing S3 report and route corrective findings to management. - Decision owner: Deterministic validators/evaluation routines, configured model selector or human comparator within the same adaptation process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Harness component checks, anonymous candidate selection, result reports and user-triggered eval. - Closure path: Candidate/result evidence → within-loop selection and optional later quality measurement; no complementary audit → binding whole-current corrective return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Independent improver proposals and blind selection reduce some bias, but are routine production/evaluation, not an independent S3 oversight path. - Evidence: selection.py, eval.py, judge.py. - Basis: Scoped negative distinguishing quality checks from complementary audit independence. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A separate external QA team could supply such audit for a deployed agent but is outside this reviewed system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model-backed across-task prospective harness adaptation with a second, explicitly supported parent-controlled blind-selection mode that changes which candidate and lesson enter the future task library. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar project tasks, newly observed errors and weak transfer of task-specific improvements to later tasks; user may also disagree with automated candidate choice. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Automatic selector chooses the adopted candidate and reflector extracts a transferable lesson; in --selector human mode the user personally selects among blinded final candidates while a model reflector can distill the adopted result. The choice feeds subsequent tasks through the library. - Decision owner: A: model-backed selector and reflector/proposer in CLI sweep; P: operator at the shipped interactive selector, not the benchmark author or external research institute. Each mode has separately attributable adoption authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-domain sequential Library, selectionpool, anonymous candidate files, human TTY prompt/choice, adopted tree and experience persistence, context.build injection into later proposer contexts; final project deployment is optional with apply. - Closure path: Encounters and candidate execution evidence → automatic/human chosen task harness → model-reflected generalizable lesson → modelib.add → next task&#39;s proposal context includes earlier domain library → potential changed subsequent harness. After a run, generated consolidated harness may also be explicitly applied to project files. - Boundary reachability: Shipped cli/run.py chooses HumanSelector when selector.channel.kind == human; otherwise it instantiates model-backed Proposer. Both invoke schedule.runsweep and selection.runselection; schedule.runonemode calls reflectadoption and conditionally modelib.add, while next task&#39;s runstage loads the library. cli/apply.py separately permits reviewed real project-file adoption. - External distinction: Actual newly encountered user task domains and contrasted coding-agent trajectories expose operational shortcomings not specified in a fixed harness. - Future / prospective distinction: Per-domain transferable lessons plus consolidated project harness target future tasks, rather than merely grading or repairing one immediate deliverable. - Adaptation option generated: Model editor proposes changed skills/instructions/scripts/hooks; model/human selector adopts a tested candidate; reflector writes future-reuse cues; consolidation proposes project-wide next-task harness. - Path back into current capability / S3: Selected experience is persisted in the per-domain library and read by subsequent model proposer sessions, changing the options considered for later edits. The optional apply command installs consolidated or one-task adopted components into the user&#39;s project; no independent S3 manager is asserted. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A has agent-owned candidate choice and lesson generation with model decisions returning through first-party memory to later operations. The distinct P mode assigns the same consequential selection right to the user through a real terminal interaction and returns it through the same library. This does not assign the separately guarded final installation to the model. - Evidence: run.py, human.py, selection.py, schedule.py, library.py, apply.py. - Basis: The two first-party shipped prospective adaptation loops share the persistent future-facing library, but have distinct actual adoption decision owners. - Confidence: High for reachable branch/closure, medium for model generalization since no model runs were made. - Caveats: --selector human requires a TTY and multiple finalists (k &gt; 1); at k=1 selection is trivial. A failed reflection yields no transferable lesson. Project apply defaults to human approval and is not evidence of autonomous project deployment.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No legitimate ultimate identity/mission-policy adjudication is shipped for the overall improvement organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Operator task selection, allowed editable files, model/provider choice, benchmark scope and safety limits bound execution but do not create a separate ultimate policy decision loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: User chooses and configures the run and can approve/force project-file writes; no identity-level conflict is escalated to a legitimate authority and returned as governing policy. - Decision owner: External developer/project owner for the overall purpose; shipped runtime for validation and configured model for bounded edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI config and setup, component tree validator, source boundaries, apply confirmation and git-dirty checks. - Closure path: User-provided goal and constraints → local optimization and optional installation; absent system identity dispute → authority judgment → returned revised governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Supervised application is an operational/capability adoption gate, not by itself an identity- or ethos-level S5 decision. The parent-mode S4 classification does not automatically imply S5=P. - Evidence: settings.py, run.py, apply.py. - Basis: Bounded negative for identity-level authority and operational return. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The user&#39;s broader software organization may have S5; this repository does not establish its governance authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335/assessments/ha/harness-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b4570aa846bcb5c7e3c02ccabec88b26877d4335\">b4570aa &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-26T18:56:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LearnHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-26\">2026-10-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/amosblomqvist/learnharness/tree/3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3dc88472eea41eed0d5e5f29c2f16f8d063c415b\">3dc8847 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform goal-directed teaching, research, demonstration construction and visual/PDF assistance under a chosen lesson mission. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain starting competence, unexpected wrong answers, unfamiliar facts, tool failures and domain-specific problems requiring outside evidence or visualization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Active prober/teacher models choose questions, explanation revisions, quiz checks, graph lookup and specialist delegation; specialist models choose searches, fetches, demo edits/tests and visual construction steps. - Decision owner: Shipped real Agent and runSubagent loops invoked through first-party models and tools; mere persistence/UI are not S1 decision owners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-turn persisted reconstruction, probe-to-teach seam, schema-validated question/plan tools, model selection and BYOK gates, structured worker toolsets, source-specific job persistence and abort handling. - Closure path: Learner goal/response → prober or teacher model decides action → asks/teaches/checks, or dispatches a specialist model → learner answers or worker returns evidence/artifact → teacher uses new information in following action. - Boundary reachability: handleChatRequest constructs buildAgent for the prober/teacher and jobTools dispatches to real shipped subagent runners; explicit configured model keys are needed. Scripted test engines do not supply the credited autonomy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model agents select semantically different learning questions, explanation paths, search/fix iterations and specialist output; fixed question grading and JSON work queues alone cannot choose these. - Evidence: teacher runtime, job tool dispatch, researcher with web tools, demo builder with real headless check. - Basis: Shipped, model-backed adaptive operation and separately executable agent specialties. - Confidence: High source-path, medium live-provider efficacy (not executed). - Caveats: BYOK and required tool capabilities are deployment prerequisites. Tests use fake engines and do not validate pedagogical outcomes with real users.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Would attenuate a conflict/oscillation specifically between independent lesson S1 workers and feed a revised coordination commitment into those workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing background job completions, shared graph writes, multiple specialist reports or two S1 units reaching incompatible conclusions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Per-chat wake/steer deduplication and global graph audit lock serialize events; the teacher commissions specialists and receives their reports. No first-party autonomous inter-specialist disagreement negotiation or adjusted joint commitments is shown. - Decision owner: Deterministic job registry and semaphore control the mechanics; teacher handles the educational consequence as its S3/current-lesson decision, not a distinct inter-S1 S2 arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-chat serialized wake delivery and recovery, durable pending result IDs, global limited concurrency, source-local extraction single-flight and global serialized audit/merge. - Closure path: Queued worker completion is delivered to teacher once; two graph audits cannot concurrently read and write a stale graph. This is mechanical admission/stability without a distinctive semantic response returned to the conflicting S1 workers. - Boundary reachability: The named locks, duplicate IDs, consent gates and worker result steers are live first-party source paths, but no separate model mediator resolves S1-vs-S1 work collision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Correct sequencing protects state, yet methodology requires specific inter-S1 disturbance, distinct discretionary response and returned adjustment of S1 behaviour, not delegation or a queue alone. - Evidence: serialized delivery / wake protocol, graph audit gate and source single-flight, six-system topology. - Basis: Bounded negative for agent-owned inter-S1 conflict attenuation; credible structural serialization is not enough. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: A user can ask the teacher to reconcile contradictory reports; no dedicated shipped inter-S1 coordination mandate is thereby established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Keep one current learning session coherent across the learner&#39;s goal, assessed understanding, dependency plan, teaching steps and commissioned specialist work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Learner confusion invalidating current plan assumptions, wrong facts, delayed external support, mismatch between learning target and work in progress, specialist failures and changed user constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The teacher has the current session transcript and goal, authors/revises the visible plan, decides which knowledge gap matters now, allocates research/demo/visual/PDF work and changes explanation/check sequence based on returned worker results and learner answers. - Decision owner: Model-bearing first-party teacher for the session-local teaching mission; learner can change the overall request, but deterministic planinit/queue enforcement is not the author of a new plan or delegation decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Prober handoff, current session record, DAG validator, question node tags, job input and result steers, durable wake, one-turn active registry, disabled-tool and model-role settings. - Closure path: Prober establishes starting point → teacher builds global lesson dependency tree → learner&#39;s answers or independent job results reveal a current gap → teacher revises explanation/plan and initiates/cancels/uses supporting work → next quiz/lesson state reflects that decision. - Boundary reachability: buildAgent(&quot;teacher&quot;) is attached after the real probedone handoff, has plan and job tool access in the supported chat pipeline; background worker result messages return to this same persistent session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: It is more than a blind router: the teacher decides a session-wide pedagogical priority and changes ongoing commitment to the goal in response to actual feedback. It does not manage the entire multi-session application, unrelated practice sets or a global provider budget. - Whole-system current view: Session transcript, learner goal, active plan/progress, commissioned worker result steers, and optional read-only graph inspection relevant to the session. - Current-control decision scope: Revise dependency plan/teaching sequence, task requests to multiple agents and response to produced evidence within the declared one-session recursion. - Evidence: teacher planning and on-the-fly repair, tool registry and model loop, worker feedback into live teacher, job instruction and correction. - Basis: Source-closed current session-wide semantic decision/return using autonomous teacher. Not a claim of product-wide S3 at higher recursion. - Confidence: Medium; stronger for declared one-session recursion than a broader multi-session organization. - Caveats: Plan progress tags are deterministic, not a second autonomous controller. No source proof of reliable cognitive diagnosis is inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Reconcile the identity/novelty claim of graph propositions extracted from the session against separately read existing graph evidence before committing the update. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate concepts minted by distinct extraction runs or different sessions, same fact paraphrased as different graph nodes, or identity links based on stale graph snapshots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A distinct model auditor explores the existing graph through find/listsessions/listpropositions and submits links; mergeFindings uses those judgments under the audit lock. It cannot inspect or fact-check the raw teaching source. - Decision owner: Separate first-party auditor agent makes semantic identity judgments, with deterministic serialization and merge applying them. No independent raw-source verification or challenge to extractor factual truth is present, so classify a narrow complementary constructor C, not general-purpose closed independent S3 A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Schema-bounded auditor submit, graph exploration tools, process-wide FIFO audit/merge lock, deterministic ledger writes, coverage checkpoints, rollback-on-stage-failure and source provenance. - Closure path: Extractor proposes lesson propositions → isolated auditor sees proposed propositions plus independently queried existing graph → returns identity links → merger collapses duplicate concepts or adds new ones → teacher can subsequently query the updated graph; no verified correction of teaching claims is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Enabled graph extraction invokes runAuditor in the standard source-to-ledger pipeline and mergeFindings consumes its output; BYOK extractor/auditor keys are required. It is a mandatory same-path graph integrity check, not a standing out-of-band lesson review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The auditor selects identity links through model exploration rather than code-only deduplication, but its complementary scope is narrow and the raw-source evidence boundary excludes stronger autonomous verification claims. - Claim being audited: Extractor&#39;s implicit assertion that newly proposed graph propositions are unique rather than semantic duplicates of an existing concept. - Ordinary reporting path: Extractor&#39;s submitted proposition list and source-local digest, subsequently represented as new graph nodes. - Complementary access path: Separate graph-aware model agent gets the live existing graph through first-party find/list tools while the extractor is isolated from that graph&#39;s full cross-source state. - Independence boundary: Distinct agent role, prompt and tools; no access to original source text, same application and possible underlying provider model; must not be confused with epistemic truth-checking. - Who acts on findings: The deterministic merger applies links immediately; the resulting graph is read-only exposed to future teacher sessions and the learner. - Evidence: auditor mandate and source exclusion, independent graph exploration tools, graph merge/evidence ledger. - Basis: Active model-backed narrow identity-audit constructor with complementary graph access; no independently validated correctness of the input claims. - Confidence: Medium for S3 constructor, high for its explicit limitations. - Caveats: Headless demo-browser tests occur inside the producing S1 worker and are not independent S3. A routine verifier in the same ingest path should not be promoted to a comprehensive audit of the lesson; future independent same-ref review could reasonably bound this to — if graph identity verification is considered inseparable production validation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Would scout changing external/future learning or agent capabilities and return options that alter the lesson organization&#39;s capability rather than just answer a current fact question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future model/tool shifts, curriculum/environment changes and novel teaching methods outside the present learner request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Researcher retrieves outside evidence only for teacher&#39;s current informational need; learner/operator selects provider, prompt override and enabled tools by settings. No standing future-oriented strategy process is shown. - Decision owner: Teacher for current accuracy and human for manually changed app settings, not a distinct autonomous S4 opportunity/threat agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Search/fetch researcher, durable cross-session knowledge graph, provider roles, prompt/settings UI and saved learner records. - Closure path: External research returns a cited brief into the current lesson and can correct facts, while persistent graph can inform later sessions; neither source path generates and adopts a prospective capability-change strategy. - Boundary reachability: Actual researcher and settings/graph surfaces exist; no future-option-to-S3 contract is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fetching recent facts and cumulative memory do not themselves create an outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: researcher prompt and web tools, teacher research requirement, global model settings. - Basis: Bounded negative: external information serves the current session instead of strategic adaptation. - Confidence: High for reviewed standard path. - Caveats: A user may ask about future trends as a lesson topic; the topic alone does not make its executor S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Would resolve an ultimate identity/purpose legitimacy conflict for the learning organization with binding authority and return a governing policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting fundamental educational goals or competing legitimacy claims rather than everyday disagreement about the next lesson step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Learner chooses or changes the study goal and app configuration; teacher&#39;s instructions prescribe a method and ask the learner to approve the plan. No standing binding constitutional-purpose adjudication is wired. - Decision owner: Human learner sets the mission external to the autonomous lesson, teacher regulates subordinate pedagogical commitments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Probe/goal question, approval before teaching, role prompts, per-user provider and prompt settings. - Closure path: Learner can change the study target, and the next session turn adapts. This is ordinary task definition and plan review, not an adjudicated fundamental identity-policy dispute. - Boundary reachability: Goal elicitation and plan confirmation are active, but identity-level disagreement resolution and binding organizational policy are not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A mandatory teaching philosophy in a system prompt is static implementation policy, not runtime S5 authority. - Evidence: probe/teach split, teacher&#39;s plan confirmation, fixed teaching identity. - Basis: Bounded negative for first-party policy legitimacy closure. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: A learner remains the legitimate authority to select its task, but task selection does not on its own justify S5=P at the present recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903/assessments/le/learnharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e0377deccfa4315a6ad15781c29c8fa36bea6903\">e0377de &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T15:18:43+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">exo</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Moissanite-Labs/exo/tree/e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e9ad895d2c59e998f0358b982a9b8a4ac0ad2aea\">e9ad895 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: persistent coding/assistant work with discretionary tool use and delegated subtasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying tasks, repository state, tool/provider errors and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose tools, edits, commands and follow-up steps from current observations. - Decision owner: the main or configured delegated agent in the first-party Loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CoreEngine composition, router, tool runner, plugins/approval desk, context tree and typed result persistence. - Closure path: tool results are appended to messages before the next model round; steering and errors update subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: compose.create builds the current core, registers the delegator, connects runtool and starts Loop; no legacy Hermes host is required for this claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves tool/transport/persistence mechanisms without task-level transformation decisions. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/loop.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider APIs/claude-cli can supply inference; their unrelated harness functions are not borrowed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no bounded first-party inter-S1 coordination function established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Generic child concurrency and shared-log consistency do not establish a concrete inter-S1 interference/attenuation/feedback witness. The writer lock excludes competing cores of the same persistent agent, not mutually adjusted S1 work cells; peer messaging/delegation alone is insufficient. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/core/agentlog.py; src/exo/peers.py; src/exo/core/engine.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current organizational control decision loop established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task decomposition, narrow task intervention and constructor-selected limits do not independently establish whole-system resource/commitment regulation. The reviewed commands expose current state but no additional function-specific S3 decision/return path beyond ordinary operations was evidenced; no P inferred from a generic operator UI. - Evidence: src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/subagents.py; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/modelspend/policy.py; src/exo/scheduler.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent complementary audit loop established for current operational claims. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the function-specific disturbance described below is not regulated by a shipped organizational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no function-specific decisive organizational right established. - Decision owner: not established; runtime mechanisms retain their narrower implementation role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the inspected task, lifecycle or configuration mechanisms below. - Closure path: no function-specific returned decision into subsequent regulation established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Trace and findings are first-party observability/self-report with deterministic rechecks; generic critic delegation lacks an audit-specific claim/access/corrective contract. Learning critique and held-out adaptation testing concern prospective proposals, not a separately closed operational audit. No S3 is inferred from audit/reviewer terminology. - Evidence: docs/TRACE.md; src/exo/core/engine.py; src/exo/core/findings.py; src/exo/core/tools/builtin/harness.py; src/exo/core/agents/delegate.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/climb.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Bounded to the pinned supported distribution; hypothetical downstream extensions are not assessed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: adapt future behavior to the external principal&#39;s recurring needs and corrected preferences through typed, evidence-linked options. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current behavior no longer matches principal corrections/rulings or generalizable class-of-task expectations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: derive a beyond-instance lesson and adopt an eligible future behavior rule; alternatively the principal explicitly accepts/rejects a proposed adaptation. - Decision owner: base mode: aux reviewer/critic supplies adaptive judgment and bounded automatic promotion closes eligible ordinary lessons. Parent mode: legitimate store operator/principal decides the concrete proposal. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal authorship filtering, evidence/session/weight constraints, guarded/conflict holds, proposal diffs, promotion ledger and refreshed memory nodes. - Closure path: eligible autonomous promotions or explicit proposalaccept write accepted nodes/skills and ledger state; exo-memory reads nodes fresh for subsequent turns. Guarded/skill paths do not silently bypass their gates. - Boundary reachability: compose attaches learn to the current engine; onturnend starts review for new principal signals; review calls constrained applypolicy. CLI exo proposals exposes show/accept/reject, and accepted nodes return through installed memory. Optional slow API/fixture export is not the basis of automatic A. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model develops the prospective behavioral lesson from external principal feedback; deterministic policy constrains its acceptance. In the explicit review mode the principal owns adoption. Mere transcript retention, fact recall or a roadmap is not the credited S4 witness. - Evidence: src/exo/compose.py; src/exo/learn/learner.py; src/exo/learn/review.py; src/exo/learn/promote.py; src/exo/consolidate.py; docs/LEARNING.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: base claim is limited to enabled store-backed, eligible generalizable behavior lessons; facts stored without a prospective behavior implication are not S4. Skill/held-out nightly automatic scheduling is not claimed; no empirical effectiveness or transfer result.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain ultimate authority over the harness&#39;s public/irreversible/financial risk-policy boundaries at the principal recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runtime gate policy changes could relax reserved authority or alter ultimate allowed exposure/risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: adopt or withhold a changed governing gate policy, rather than merely approve one action. - Decision owner: the authenticated live principal; agent/peer input cannot supply that credential. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: principal transport/authorship checks, pinned policy snapshot, changed-policy hashes, stricter interim enforcement and signed acceptance record. - Closure path: gate status exposes changed policy; /gate accept requires principal authority, records policyaccept and replaces the pinned policy used to govern later calls. - Boundary reachability: installed exo-gate command routes accept to acceptpolicies; the current composed core loads plugins and carries principal authority through the transport path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ultimate policy adoption remains with the principal; deterministic classification, hashes and individual exact-text approval are supporting controls, not autonomous S5. - Evidence: plugins/exo-gate/exogate/hook.py; src/exo/principal.py; src/exo/compose.py; docs/GATE.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: bounded to the legitimate principal&#39;s ultimate risk/delegation policy; no claim that every approval, SOUL.md edit or maintainer action is S5. Required-executor behavior is mode-specific. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: who may authorize public/irreversible/financial acts and whether changed reserved-risk policy becomes governing. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: principal transport authority checked by requireprincipal; no internal autonomous S5 mode established. - Return-to-operation path: changed policy status/hash → live principal accept → logged policyaccept → new pinned policy → subsequent tool decisions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e/assessments/mo/moissanite-labs-exo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 37735444207e5ea17cd166d25eaa1dde9259c22e\">3773544 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T18:29:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openbin-ai/binny\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BINNY</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/openbin-ai/binny/tree/d0c2ae4c61d222421d1505986ac6e63b7ed799fa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d0c2ae4c61d222421d1505986ac6e63b7ed799fa\">d0c2ae4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous reverse-engineering investigation and evidence-backed draft construction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown binary behaviour, contradictory strings/function evidence, tool failure, and incomplete classification or report sections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model chooses evidence queries and investigation tool calls, revises hypotheses from results, and authors findings/report sections. - Decision owner: The model actor inside the shipped Engine.Step loop, not deterministic phase transitions or the remote provider&#39;s service operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool registry, static sandbox, STIX/playbook prompts, objective tracking, token caps, context compaction and optional human checkpoints. - Closure path: Model call→first-party tool execution→result in e.messages→subsequent model choice; stages culminate in reviewable draft sections and findings. - Boundary reachability: The TUI&#39;s ordinary investigation command constructs the first-party engine with provider/tool registry and runs Step; no developer-authored coordinator is necessary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Runtime selects budgets and legal tool boundaries, while only model reasoning selects semantically relevant operations against the sample. - Evidence: Engine Step; stage transitions; tool dispatch. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for shipped loop architecture. - Caveats: No live third-party model calls or analyst-domain correctness tests were conducted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No distinct inter-S1 coordination function is shipped at this one-investigation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool sequence and triage/investigate phase changes belong to one S1, not conflict between independent operational cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party ownership/adjustment of competing S1 work is exposed. - Decision owner: None for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Stage machine, token caps, project state and report objectives. - Closure path: Tool and phase feedback returns to the same model actor; no inter-unit attenuation return exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct sequential phases or API tools are not distinct S1 units. - Evidence: engine, README. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for the reviewed distribution. - Caveats: Separate analysts can run independent processes, without a shipped cross-run coordination system.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-current management of distinct operational commitments/resources is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Per-task token limits, phase checkpoints and model refusals are local investigation constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No whole-current bargaining/reallocation or cross-S1 intervention right is present. - Decision owner: Analyst selects autonomy/budgets and the engine enforces caps; model selects local evidence tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: maxRounds, maxTokens, phase-gates, /autonomy and ContinuePastCap. - Closure path: Cap reached→local pause/analyst extension or stop; no S3 return into an operational whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Budget enforcement and human override of one analysis are not a distinct S3 function or parent-governed S3 control. - Evidence: engine caps, TUI controls. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high at the selected recursion. - Caveats: Do not infer OpenBin enterprise project management from this CLI&#39;s project selector.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Assertions in a previously produced sample report about functions, addresses, reachability and malware/vulnerability findings. - Ordinary reporting path: The investigation model records findings and report sections during triage/investigate/synthesize, with ordinary tool results in its own context. - Complementary access path: StartQA(report) creates an on-demand skeptical review context, loads report content as claims to check rather than facts and directs fresh raw function/string/xref/reachability reads against source evidence. - Independence boundary: The Q&amp;A mode does not inherit the original evidence as trusted prior work; the model must inspect independently retrieved sample evidence. It is procedural/mode separation, not a separate institution or mandatory different provider. - Who acts on findings: Review-model feedback returns to the analyst; analyst can request recorded correction and /save a revised draft, or use the returned negative evidence for later investigation. - Function: Complementary source-based challenge of ordinary report claims beyond trusting their original narrative. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but false analysis claims, unsupported reachability or section assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In explicitly initiated verification, the model judges the actual sampled evidence as supporting/weakening/refuting a report claim. - Decision owner: Q&amp;A model actor for evidence judgment; analyst owns case selection and final report publication, engine just transports tool results and report edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: qaKickoff skeptical prompt, source read tools, report snapshot and draft editing/saving. - Closure path: Analyst asks to verify→separately started Q&amp;A model reads source→returns a specific finding→analyst may direct corrections/write findings→draft /save returns corrected evidence into the project&#39;s output. - Boundary reachability: /ask and StartQA are shipped user-facing operational modes, not a test fixture or maintainer-only check. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model determines whether primary evidence falsifies the report; deterministic tools merely expose bytes/functions. This credits optional agent-owned audit judgment, not autonomous selection of audit targets or remediation. - Evidence: StartQA; skeptical Q&amp;A instruction; README /ask. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium for optional complementary review topology. - Caveats: No automatic audit after every draft, provider isolation attestation, or validated remediation was observed; audit depends on an analyst asking concrete questions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No future-oriented external environmental sensing→adaptation options→present capability return is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New malware class or vulnerability questions are investigated as the current deliverable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No standing prospective redesign of BINNY&#39;s own repertoire from environmental trends is exposed. - Decision owner: Developers select playbooks/releases; model picks current-case observations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: STIX classification, playbook selection, provider switching and state persistence. - Closure path: Classifier selects an existing playbook for the current sample; this is S1 task steering, not a forward capability-adaptation loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Planning and classifying the current case do not constitute externally informed S4 intelligence. - Evidence: playbooks, engine stages. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: An analyst may use results to change future practices externally; that arrangement is outside the assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure for the agent harness is not supplied as a distinct organizational process. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool risk, sample access, report publication and model-provider configuration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No legitimate ultimate mission/policy dispute and returned binding decision path is present. - Decision owner: Analyst/developer selects mode and tool permissions; ordinary report approval is not ultimate authority over identity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sandbox restrictions, API auth, prompts, report draft gate and runtime settings. - Closure path: Permission or save choices affect current case execution/publication; no identity-level governance return is shown. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static security/configuration and one draft approval do not establish S5, including the parent mode. - Evidence: README, sandbox, TUI. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is not an assessment of the upstream organization or platform policies.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f849711e2035564b9de8aafcd766be6a15588b15/assessments/bi/binny.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f849711e2035564b9de8aafcd766be6a15588b15\">f849711 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-29T10:48:20+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josh-j/pwsh-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PwshHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-29\">2026-10-29</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/josh-j/pwsh-harness/tree/e56e989bbd68d1e6a1138c3755d6c76266700a78\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e56e989bbd68d1e6a1138c3755d6c76266700a78\">e56e989 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-owned workspace operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing task requirements, file content, tool failures and shell feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select tool/arguments and revise subsequent actions or final answer. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed parent agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapter, tool registry, session lease, exact-action Approve or activated Auto, budget and logging enforcement. - Closure path: Tool result enters the model exchange; the next model request decides continuation; final answer and session records retain outcome. - Boundary reachability: Start-PwshHarness enables shipped agent mode; native tools and continuation are installed by the bundled agent extension. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without the model the dispatcher and policies retain limits but do not choose task-specific edits and repairs. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTools.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1 - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for code wiring - Caveats: PowerShell 7.6/provider execution was not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Inter-S1 interference regulation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Read-only subagents run one at a time; shell background tasks are subprocess tools. Plans, ordered calls and session exclusivity do not demonstrate distinct operational work cells mutually adjusting a concrete conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Read-only subagents run one at a time; shell background tasks are subprocess tools. Plans, ordered calls and session exclusivity do not demonstrate distinct operational work cells mutually adjusting a concrete conflict. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentWork.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTasks.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-harness current control is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Token/cost/time reservations, retry fallback and user steering regulate one task under supplied limits; a model does not acquire whole-system resource/commitment authority from those gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Token/cost/time reservations, retry fallback and user steering regulate one task under supplied limits; a model does not acquire whole-system resource/commitment authority from those gates. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/Budget.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementary challenge of a reported source defect before publication. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A parent model can mistake a guard/caller/library premise and publish a false or unsupported finding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge whether the concrete claimed defect is supported, refuted, reproduced or unresolved after direct source/counterevidence inspection. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only checker model invoked by reportfinding; runtime parses/records the judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bounded readonly schemas, fresh provider exchange, exact file/line/trigger/consequence, observed-command provenance and completion policy. - Closure path: Invoke-AgentFindingCheck returns verdict/premise/counterevidence to the parent; Get-AgentFindingReview rejects citing refuted findings and requests checked/qualified reporting in a further step. - Boundary reachability: Bundled AgentTools installs work schemas and completion-review; AgentPresentation dispatches reportfinding to Invoke-AgentFindingCheck; Engine Delegate creates the separate read-only exchange. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model tests the disputed premise using callers/tests/guards, while deterministic parsing and answer reminders only enforce its evidence labels. Generic logs and routine parser/Pester checks are not credited. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentWork.ps1; Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentLoop.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentTools.ps1; Extensions/agent/AgentPresentation.ps1 - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for this bounded audit mode - Caveats: Parent selects the claim and both actors may share provider/model. Independence is fresh reasoning with separate direct source access, not institutional independence; no live witness or universal claim coverage. - Claim being audited: Parent&#39;s reported defect at File/Line with Trigger/Consequence and optional executed reproduction. - Ordinary reporting path: Parent&#39;s own source interpretation and draft final finding. - Complementary access path: Fresh checker reads cited code, callers, guards and tests through independently selected readonly tools and searches for refutation. - Independence boundary: New base messages and separate provider cache/replay, readonly actor, no nested delegates; it receives the claim rather than the parent&#39;s full reasoning transcript. - Who acts on findings: Parent model revises/qualifies its finding; completion-review returns contradictions into subsequent operation when a step/time budget remains.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: External/prospective capability adaptation is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Lessons summarize past workspace command success; /extend implements a directly requested extension. Neither path supplies environmental/future modelling that develops and selects an adaptation option. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lessons summarize past workspace command success; /extend implements a directly requested extension. Neither path supplies environmental/future modelling that develops and selects an adaptation option. - Evidence: Extensions/agent/AgentLessons.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Commands.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Authoring.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Identity/ultimate-policy closure is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Live Auto activation, source-bound extension approval and risk capability labels constrain ordinary task/extension execution without addressing system identity or balancing S3/S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-specific decision/return path identified. - Decision owner: No credited metasystem owner; developer/operator configuration and agent task decisions remain separate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session leases, run policies, budget enforcement, logs and ordinary approvals. - Closure path: No qualifying function-specific closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Live Auto activation, source-bound extension approval and risk capability labels constrain ordinary task/extension execution without addressing system identity or balancing S3/S4. - Evidence: Modules/PwshHarness.Engine/Private/AgentAuthority.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Approval.ps1; Extensions/self-extend/Commands.ps1 - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/17bf101ef55129f7f992cd21e1a1cf5b49b9b154/assessments/pw/pwshharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 17bf101ef55129f7f992cd21e1a1cf5b49b9b154\">17bf101 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:23:45+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oswald-Agent/Oswald\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OSWALD</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Oswald-Agent/Oswald/tree/5a2d6103c4c48735a909fd5ee78b0362fcbee993\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a2d6103c4c48735a909fd5ee78b0362fcbee993\">5a2d610 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute software-change work by interpreting a task, inspecting context, modifying code, generating/testing acceptance evidence and deciding whether the resulting patch is adequate. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable user requests and codebases, code/tool/test failures, ambiguous specifications and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner&#39;s task plan, Coder&#39;s source edits and next tool calls, Tester&#39;s test construction/execution, and Reviewer&#39;s acceptance or blocking critique within the task. - Decision owner: distinct provider-backed role agents invoked in the first-party shipped CLI; model responses choose content and tool actions. The deterministic orchestrator bounds the lifecycle; the human still owns final external publication. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable worktree base, role-scoped tools, sandbox, artifact schema/persistence, stage timeout and two-attempt cap. - Closure path: Planner spec → Coder model/tool edits → Tester authored/executed tests → Reviewer model verdict → success or findings returned to Coder for a second attempt, or reported stop with saved patch. - Boundary reachability: the default local CLI explicitly constructs all four agent modules and runs them through Orchestrator; it is not an example-only or future-service path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: plans, edits, tests and review judgments are model-authored; deterministic stage gates implement those decisions rather than originate task-specific discretion. - Evidence: CLI roles, Planner model/tools, Coder tool loop, Tester commands, orchestrator corrective feedback. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for shipped source and decision path; no live LLM/provider run performed. - Caveats: fake test adapters and architecture diagrams do not attest to model quality or autonomous PR publication; operator must separately inspect/apply the saved patch.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no distinct inter-S1 interference-attenuation loop established at the declared one-task boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: compatibility between implementation, tests and review comments, rather than independently competing S1 units with an explicit oscillation/conflict regulation relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: role outputs and correction findings are routed in a fixed sequence; no separately established S2 right mediates a specific inter-unit disturbance. - Decision owner: role agents own stage-level S1 choices and the deterministic pipeline sequences handoffs. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: typed artifact handoffs, stage ordering, test/review gates and bounded retry. - Closure path: a failed test or blocked review returns to the Coder inside the same task loop, not to two or more independently active S1 units through S2 coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: four named agents and a correction edge are not evidence of a separate interference-attenuation function; no first-party inter-S1 mutual adjustment witness is supplied. - Evidence: fixed pipeline and retry, role binding. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported local single-task CLI. - Caveats: separately operated Oswald processes or future web workers are outside the reviewed first-party S2 boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no first-party whole-system discretionary current-control loop over a portfolio of operational commitments/resources is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stage failure, timeout, unapproved code, worktree and sandbox cleanup, not dynamic whole-system resource allocation or accountable priority conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: stage progression, retry and stop conditions are fixed in code; model actors can only make task-stage decisions; the operator controls the invocation and external publication. - Decision owner: developer predefines lifecycle/limits; orchestrator enforces them; human retains final integration decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run status/metrics, stagetimeout, two-attempt correction, sandbox isolation, report persistence, cancellation and cleanup. - Closure path: deterministic gate/stop and report/patch return, not a discretionary whole-system current-control decision returning to alter a multi-S1 operational portfolio. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the central Orchestrator is named like a manager but its current control is a fixed single-task state machine; progress telemetry does not transfer whole-system regulatory authority to an agent. - Evidence: lifecycle/limits, stop, retry and status, recorded run/metrics. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for current CLI boundary. - Caveats: human patch review and planned fleet services are not S3 parent modes in the shipped pipeline.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently examine the Coder&#39;s proposed source changes and Tester evidence for acceptance against a specification, with a blocking corrective verdict. - Disturbance / variety regulated: silent implementation defects, self-reported completion or tests that fail to support claimed acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separate Reviewer model chooses APPROVED, CHANGESREQUESTED or rejection with blocking findings against the cumulative source diff, plan and tests. - Decision owner: ReviewerAgent model in the shipped role wiring; the orchestrator validates the verdict and deterministically applies the gate, rather than authoring review judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh manager.getdiff() captured after Tester edits, immutable reviewchanges artifact, read-only Reviewer tool boundary, typed verdict consistency checks and correction cap. - Closure path: cumulative worktree diff plus test evidence → distinct read-only Reviewer model judgment → orchestrator veto or returned findings → Coder correction attempt/terminal fail, with review and patch saved for human inspection. - Boundary reachability: the default CLI always assembles and invokes ReviewerAgent after actual test execution; the diff is freshly read from the worktree, not merely accepted from the Coder&#39;s own completion narrative. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Reviewer produces substantive model-authored blocking issues, while deterministic checks reject internally inconsistent approvals; authoring and audit roles have different inputs/permissions. - Claim being audited: Coder&#39;s source change satisfies the Planner&#39;s specified work and is supported by tests. - Ordinary reporting path: Coder-produced CodeChanges and Tester-produced TestResults passed into the main pipeline. - Complementary access path: direct cumulative worktree diff via WorktreeManager.getdiff() and separate read-only model inspection of spec, changes and testresults. - Independence boundary: Reviewer is a distinct model invocation/role with no write or tool permissions, evaluates actual patch and test artifacts rather than relying solely on Coder status; not an external independent reviewer, separate provider, or independently reexecuted tests. - Who acts on findings: the orchestrator returns review blocks to Coder for at most one correction, or refuses success and preserves the terminal report. - Evidence: fresh diff and Reviewer gate, read-only model-authored verdict, reviewer composition. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium for installed complementary review/feedback topology; no real provider run or measured audit efficacy. - Caveats: audit sees produced test results and patch rather than obtaining separate runtime probes; independence is role/access separation at source level, not an organizationally independent third-party semantic review. No automatic merge authority follows.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no external/prospective sensing → adaptation choice → durable current-capability change loop demonstrated in the supported CLI. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unpredictable current software task and its failing tests are handled in S1, not future technology/environment adaptation of Oswald itself. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Planner chooses task implementation approach, not a strategic future capability portfolio; no S4 adaptation owner or returned decision is wired. - Decision owner: user selects tasks and maintainers evolve Oswald outside assessed runtime; the agent pipeline cannot promote its patch into Oswald capability autonomously. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task plan, correction findings, saved reports/patch, model-provider configuration and developer roadmap. - Closure path: within-task corrections may improve a candidate patch but do not produce an external-and-future adaptation judgment applied to the harness&#39;s current operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: code planning on a supplied request and a human-applied patch are local S1 work, not S4; planned architecture is adjacent. - Evidence: Planner request boundary, no-publish pipeline, future architecture disclaimer. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for supported local mode. - Caveats: third-party users can ask Oswald to implement strategic tooling, but this alone does not give the harness a self-directed S4 decision loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no supported runtime loop for ultimate purpose/identity-policy conflict and authoritative closure at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary task request constraints, sandbox restrictions and code publication risk, rather than identity/charter tensions between inside-and-now and outside-and-then. - Decisive decision or feedback right: task constraints are supplied by the human and enforced by runtime; no identity/ultimate-policy matter is routed to a legitimate parent decision and returned as a governing choice. - Decision owner: user/maintainers choose task, models, sandbox and external patch disposition, but their role is not a first-party closed S5 mode of the task runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: configuration, role prompts, task schema, tool access, test/review gates and human post-run patch review. - Closure path: approval/denial and failure return to the local task, not a policy-identity decision governing the future purpose of Oswald. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed prompts, sandbox/tool restrictions and a human hold before Git mutation are ordinary constraints; neither agent nor parent returns a runtime identity decision. - Evidence: CLI config and role wiring, README human VCS boundary, no commit/merge orchestration. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported CLI. - Caveats: upstream OSS maintainer governance is adjacent, and the target end-state human approval API is not credited as an installed S5 parent mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/03af5b02eb68d24c6ad4fbda9aa842fcbd4c309d/assessments/os/oswald.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 03af5b02eb68d24c6ad4fbda9aa842fcbd4c309d\">03af5b0 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-18T03:22:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Harness²</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-18\">2026-10-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/google-research/harness2/tree/1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1cfe254e8d25b15f37048c3730134686abe60335\">1cfe254 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out the present task&#39;s harness-editing and probing work through autonomous model-chosen candidate changes, actual executor rollouts and evidence-informed candidate selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unseen tasks, insufficient baseline harness behavior, different candidate tool/prompt/skill modifications, executable errors and heterogeneous task deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The configured proposer model chooses what edits to write inside the allowed harness tree; selector model chooses among concrete completed candidate outputs using execution evidence. Those chosen changes are validated and run by the shipped orchestration, with later candidate/edit choices exposed to resulting observations. - Decision owner: Model-backed Proposer/selector operating through first-party runstage, runmode and runselection; external model/runtime implementations supply inference and task execution but do not replace first-party decision/feedback routing. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python stage scheduler, editable tree validation, candidate/run budget ledger, real adapter execution, retry/fallback, evidence-safe render, filesystem result records and selection persistence. - Closure path: Task/base rollout → proposal-agent harness edit → first-party tree accept/reject → substrate probe with new tree → observed deliverable/trajectory → independent composition/selection decision → chosen current-task output and selection record. - Boundary reachability: Public CLI runsweep → rundomain → runtask → runmode → runstage/real candidate adapter execution → runselection is shipped source. Proposer and selector may be model-backed via released Proposer.call implementations rather than sample-only DemoProposer. No external task executor cognitive function is claimed as a Harness² internal actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: First-party code gives model-backed editor/selector discretion that changes candidate harnesses and selects from observed task outcomes; deterministic scheduler, validator and external benchmark judge are only enforcement/evidence mechanisms. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/proposer.py. - Basis: Reachable configured proposal/edit/probe/compare/select source loop for current task. - Confidence: High for structural mode reachability; no model-backed external benchmark execution attested. - Caveats: Model-less deterministic demo is not itself autonomous; the positive applies only to the supported configured model editor/selector with functional substrate. Absolute benchmark gains are not used as VSM classification evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No independent S2 feedback mechanism stabilizes specific interference between separately viable S1 operating units at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel task candidates and concurrent domains can compete for execution resources or diverge, but running k proposals/rollouts is controlled experimental scheduling rather than detected inter-S1 organizational conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: ThreadPoolExecutor widths, domain ordering, mode-separated namespaces and shared candidate-pool aggregation control concurrency/experiment identity. No agent makes inter-operating-division stabilization decisions that feed back to competing S1 units. - Decision owner: Deterministic task/domain scheduler and user-specified budgets/workers, not a model-owned S2 coordination actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Candidate labels, per-mode Namespace, thread pools, deterministic domain order, cost ledger and output directory partitioning. - Closure path: Proposals/rollouts execute and join → composition/selection. Missing a sensed cross-S1 interference → specific adaptive damping judgment → returned adjustments to each affected autonomous S1 operating division. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Task stages and domain jobs are parts of one harness-optimization experiment; concurrent task execution and result aggregation do not automatically establish S2 among independent viable operating organizations. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded source negative after identifying all shipped within-task and across-domain concurrency mechanisms. - Confidence: High for reviewed default modes. - Caveats: An external experiment orchestrator or multi-tenant platform could add S2, but neither is part of the assessed frozen first-party distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No distinct whole-current organization control loop assigns and revises commitments/resources/priorities over multiple independent S1 operating domains. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task failures, budgets and experimental queue pressure are managed at run scope; no whole-system operational obligations spanning independent current S1 divisions are established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scheduler selects the prespecified task order/mode/budget and handles per-task failures; model selector chooses a task artifact; neither makes a global current-control intervention among distinct operating units. - Decision owner: CLI operator for configuration, deterministic Python scheduler for execution and model editor/selector for task results. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Namespace-separated runs, BudgetLedger, result statuses, resume, checked candidate trees and domain task streaming. - Closure path: Configured tasks → bounded execution/selection/summary. Missing whole-organization status → legitimate binding current priority/resource/commitment decision → intervention returned into separate S1 operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Select-best-harness for one task is S1; allocating worker count by human CLI is configuration. Neither is an autonomous or first-party S3 decision over a viable multi-unit current organization. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/ledger.py; harness2/core/selection.py. - Basis: Boundary-relative bounded negative separating experiment management from organization current control. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Research lab/program management may be external; it is not an owned Harness² control function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary audit obtains separate operational reality evidence, challenges S3 assertions and returns authorized corrective S3 action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate harnesses can fail or produce inferior outcomes, and benchmark grader information could leak. First-party tree validation, contrastive probing and independent post-run judge help task-level evidence integrity but do not audit organizational S3 performance claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Code validates a tree and denies grader-verdict leakage; a selector judges delivered candidate outputs while an external rubric judge scores outputs in a separate stage. None reports independently sourced whole-current operating contradictions to a legitimate S3 manager with binding correction. - Decision owner: First-party task/evidence validator and choice-model for the current improvement task; external benchmark judge for scoring, not a separate audited management officer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Editable-tree validator, forbidden-grader evidence checks, candidate pool anonymization, external judgeone, cost ledger and summary artifacts. - Closure path: Candidate output/trajectory → validation and selection/benchmark score → task/result record; missing S3 current report → independent operating observation → complementary challenge → authorized corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Contrastive behavioral evidence and result-score separation are real, but output selection for one task is neither S3 evidence independence nor an audit owner with a corrective cross-system path. - Evidence: harness2/core/recursion.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/judge.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/ledger.py. - Basis: Bounded negative after explicitly considering the grader/selector/evidence-control positive candidate. - Confidence: Medium-high within the frozen app. - Caveats: A dedicated independently observable production system auditor could be built externally; no such shipped owner and returned S3 correction were shown.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Learn from current encountered environments and adopted task-level harness modifications to autonomously maintain a reusable future-facing improvement library that influences subsequent harness-design decisions across new tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open-world tasks arrive that were not known at initial harness construction; improvements for one problem may not transfer directly. Cross-task domain patterns, changing successful component choices and observed errors must inform future harness proposals. - Decisive decision or feedback right: After actual current-task rollouts, the model-backed selector picks an adopted harness from candidate evidence and model-backed reflectadoption distills transferable experience. First-party Library.add accepts that model-selected result/lesson into durable per-domain library, and subsequent proposer/editor sessions read it for future harness reconfiguration. - Decision owner: Configured autonomous selector + experience-reflecting model agent inside the shipped runtask/rundomain loop. Deterministic file/library code persists and presents the agent&#39;s chosen strategic material, not an external human deciding each adopted lesson. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Domain task stream, mode-separated Library, selected harness tree and adoption metadata, contrastive trajectory record, EXPERIENCE.md and INDEX.md, context injection, safe tree validation, leak prevention and retaining only earlier tasks. - Closure path: Environment/task rollouts → model selection of accepted harness → model distills evidence-supported transferable future lesson → library persists full adopted harness + rationale → next task imports library into new model proposer context → its operational harness edits can change under accumulated past experience. - Boundary reachability: A first-party public runsweep invokes rundomain, creates Library objects per mode/domain and executes tasks sequentially. runonemode calls reflectadoption and, with evidence-rich experience, modelib.add; runstage passes librarydir to context.build which actually copies old library context into the next proposer session. No bespoke adopter glue is needed for this supported released mode. - External distinction: Novel tasks in the same domain arrive after prior harnesses have been accepted; actual task-execution contrasts, error patterns and domain-specific cues are fed back through the prior-task experience library rather than assumed from static program configuration. - Future / prospective distinction: The reflector distils a transferable cue/lesson intended for later unknown tasks, and Library stores adopted trees and why they were chosen; this is not the immediate selected deliverable alone. - Adaptation option generated: An autonomous proposer/editor generates new harness-component edits based on stored prior domain experience, and an autonomous selector/reflection loop chooses, explains and retains the successful candidate tree and lesson to inform later alternative edits. - Path back into current capability / S3: Library.add persists the chosen tree and lesson, context.build copies the resulting library into the next proposer work directory, and recursion.runstage hands it to the model edit session whose validated candidate modifies the next task&#39;s executable harness. There is no separate whole-current S3 manager, so the concrete return is direct into next S1 harness-improvement capability at this recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent makes substantive adoption and transferable-experience decisions from observed external task outcomes; first-party memory storage/return closes them into future edit decisions. This is a prospective reusable improvement-harness capability, distinct from current task&#39;s best-output selection alone. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/selection.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/library.py; harness2/core/context.py; harness2/core/recursion.py. - Basis: Installed agent-owned adoption/reflection and persistent cross-task future-harness change feedback, independent of reported benchmark gains. - Confidence: Medium-high for the supported configured cross-task learning mode; effectiveness/generalization not independently tested. - Caveats: This is S4 of the declared harness-improvement organization, not S4 of a separately deployed fixed target coding assistant. Individual future tasks begin from a base harness but the stored adopted earlier trees and model lessons are available to the editing proposer; this is guidance-mediated capability adaptation, not unconditional promotion of a new globally deployed runtime. A failed or empty reflection prevents library admission, so not every task advances S4.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate legitimate mission/identity-policy arbitration for the harness-improvement organization is shipped as a first-party closed decision path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User-selected benchmark, base model, allowed editable components, executor, task scope and no-leak policies bound optimization but do not entail an identity/purpose governance dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: CLI/research operator fixes the task stream, model/substrate budget and source boundaries; validators enforce prechosen component constraints. No first-party actor chooses legitimate ultimate aims or changes governing ethical purpose and returns those decisions. - Decision owner: External operator/research institution for mission and boundary setup; first-party deterministic config/validation has no identity-level authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Benchmarks, CLI config, read-only rubric protection, editable-component allowlist, ledger and model/executor selection. - Closure path: Configured objective/limits → task improvements in those limits; missing legitimate ultimate policy/identity alternatives → authoritative S5 choice → returned system-wide changed governing purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Technical safety and user objective configuration are narrower than governing what the system is and what its ultimate purpose should be. - Evidence: harness2/core/schedule.py; harness2/core/spec.py; harness2/core/tree.py; harness2/core/recursion.py; docs/EXPERIMENTS.md. - Basis: Bounded no-first-party identity-purpose governance after inspecting all admissibility and external authority surfaces. - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: Google Research or the deployer may separately exercise organizational S5; not imported as a Harness² runtime ownership claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74/assessments/harness2.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a20d73e3efcee2bcc1639c7365c3b178e155f74\">9a20d73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:03:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/inhere/suga\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Suga</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/inhere/suga/tree/88916d5d23e974f7c02c9dd37f6d02f05b681a31\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 88916d5d23e974f7c02c9dd37f6d02f05b681a31\">88916d5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce coding/investigation outcomes through the model/tool loop, optionally using read-only research/exploration actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User requests, file and command results, tool refusals, provider errors and evolving task observations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select next tools, delegate exploration, interpret observations and decide the answer or next work. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed principal and its operational child actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider adapters, persistence, workspace/approval guards, transcript/context projection and limits. - Closure path: Model request → chosen tool/agent action → persisted results or child report → next model request/final answer. - Boundary reachability: Current wiring instantiates the first-party kernel and tool set for CLI/ACP; enabled agent tooling builds real read-only child runners rather than borrowing an uninstantiated framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the model actors leaves storage, permissions and transport but no task-specific discretionary choices. - Evidence: [Kernel][R1], [current wiring][R2], [child construction][R3], [agent tool][R4]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: No live inference or agent E2E execution was run. Operational quality and benchmark results are not inferred.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: The local peer-chat protocol attenuates repeated automatic replies between independent workcells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: One session&#39;s peer reply can wake another, whose response wakes the first again without new human work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the anti-reply hop policy and when a held exchange may resume. - Decision owner: The developer/operator supplies maxhops and the continuation policy; the runtime counts/holds. An autonomous coordination-policy actor is not supplied. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Sender attribution, TurnHops + 1, configured receiving threshold, held-message queue, message IDs/status and per-turn send cap. - Closure path: Peer reply advances hop depth → receiver reaches maxhops → no new turn is started → held state/status is observable and the message enters only a later human-started turn. - Boundary reachability: The shipped sessionmessage tool is available by default outside disabled/Gofer-job modes, and the real local chat socket delivery path applies the hold rule. This is a first-party anti-oscillation path, not a hypothetical custom mailbox. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model may choose a message, but the decisive coordination threshold/hold rule remains configured; C records the S2-specific construction/control surface rather than autonomous ownership. - Evidence: [Native peer tool][R9], [host message construction][R10], [hop context][R11], [receiver hold][R12], [configuration][R13]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Older peer versions may not support hop gating; the tool reports that limitation. This path does not provide shared-file reservations or coordination for arbitrary external runtimes.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the principal workgroup&#39;s current background operational commitments, including independent subagent tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Subagents, long commands or watches may remain running, fail, return unhelpful work or consume the current workgroup&#39;s time/resources after priorities change. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Review the current owned-task set, read progress/output, choose which work continues or is killed, and revise subsequent operational action. - Decision owner: Base mode: the principal model-backed agent. Parent mode: the local user supervising that same session/task organization through chat. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-scoped task manager, task snapshots/output/termination, agent run metadata, task notices, cancellation/process-tree handling and configured budget/time ceilings. - Closure path: Task list/output/notice → principal or operator selects current intervention → task kill/cancellation and resulting snapshot/notice → subsequent model work proceeds with revised commitments. - Boundary reachability: CLI/run/chat/ACP wiring installs native task controls when the first-party task manager is present; agent background runs use that manager. Chat&#39;s :tasks/-k commands directly expose the same session task state and termination path to the user. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses whether to retain or terminate current owned work; the manager only executes the choice. Configured hard limits do not own those discretionary decisions. Human supervision is separately evidenced, rather than inferred from write approval. - Evidence: [Tool current-view/intervention][R5], [manager List/Kill][R6], [model feedback][R7], [chat view/cancellation][R8], [task wiring][R23], [actor construction][R3]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Scope is one principal session&#39;s owned background workgroup. This does not establish a regulator over all independent chats or external Gofer jobs; a lone foreground run need not exercise this mode.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No first-party audit-specific independent judgment with corrective current-control return is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect completion claims, missed tests, malformed output or defects in produced artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No installed independent audit owner adjudicates an operational claim through a complementary channel. - Decision owner: The producer and configured delivery checks handle ordinary validation; explore/research children are general operational helpers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Caller-selected --verify command, fixed nudge rules, JSON-schema checks, traces, raw transcript access and read-only child tools. - Closure path: Failed delivery checks re-enter the same producing session. There is no supplied independent audit judgment/authority loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic schema/nudge/verification and generic child exploration are not complementary S3. The config/agents reviewer sketch is not one of the current tool&#39;s instantiated actor types. - Evidence: [Delivery verification][R14], [nudge][R15], [output contract][R16], [actual child types][R3], [agent interface][R4], [current/archive boundary][R21]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A caller can compose an independent reviewer around direct evidence, but that application is not supplied by the reviewed current product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No shipped external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: External research results, user feedback and long-session context pressure are operational inputs rather than a supplied future-capability model. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No intelligence actor develops adaptation alternatives and closes their return into present capability/current control. - Decision owner: The principal performs requested research and writes permitted memory; user/maintainer selects configuration and releases. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Web research child, feedback/reference memory kinds, summaries, handoff/context segments, idle consolidation and provider settings. - Closure path: Stored memory and compressed context enter later requests; no prospective adaptation-option-to-capability decision path is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Remembering corrections, summarizing history or doing a current research task does not by itself establish S4. No function-specific parent adaptation loop is evidenced. - Evidence: [Research construction][R3], [memory controls][R19], [idle consolidation][R17], [segment break][R18], [product scope][R20]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Developer optimization/evaluation and archived learning/self-loop documents are not runtime product owners.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication-and-return loop is supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Write permissions, external-origin taint, protected paths, agent instructions and memory confirmation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No identity-level matter is specifically routed to legitimate ultimate authority and returned as a governing policy decision. - Decision owner: User/developer supplies prompt, approval and tool/memory rules; agents own bounded local action choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Read-only registration, strongest approval rules, dangerous-action confirmation, taint guards, rules loading and session state. - Closure path: Allow/deny/confirm outcomes govern actions or memory writes, not ultimate identity or S3–S4 tension resolution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Static rules, user approval and a memory kind called user/feedback are not S5. The narrower user current-task supervision supports S3 only. - Evidence: [Wiring and approval assembly][R2], [child policy snapshot][R3], [memory confirmation][R19], [documented permissions][R20]. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: The public project/development organization and external Gofer governance are outside this product-level assessment.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8068db51edb8053bfef30d194229b694a2d36b55/assessments/su/suga.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8068db51edb8053bfef30d194229b694a2d36b55\">8068db5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T00:22:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/djtoon/pi-forge\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-Forge</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-31\">2026-10-31</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/djtoon/pi-forge/tree/3af0d73a0f45701a4b1324d3b3bb272bc312ef2e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3af0d73a0f45701a4b1324d3b3bb272bc312ef2e\">3af0d73 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-backed Forge and generated pi agents perform their user-delegated building or domain work by selecting tools and adapting responses to results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User demands, current workspace state, unavailable credentials, files, tool failures and environment/domain data. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide what to ask, read, write, run, validate or explain on subsequent model turns within available tools and policy. - Decision owner: The active pi model session in the supported real-provider harness run; user defines goals and guardrails. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runHarness, pi session/tool APIs, forge-tools, custom/skills, model settings, guard hooks, CLI/web/RPC transport and plan UI. - Closure path: User/domain request → model chooses tool and arguments → first-party tool executes or denies → result/observation returns to model for next step. - Boundary reachability: forge CLI launches a generated first-party harness in web/TUI and documented generated harness commands expose the same model-tool loop in headless/RPC modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Without model choice, the tools and generator can be called directly but do not decide how to fulfill an open-ended user request. - Evidence: launcher, harness core, Forge tools, interview skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Requires configured model provider; static source analysis does not certify model results, user-approval behavior or build success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party S2-specific anti-interference loop across distinct concurrently operating agent units was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Main and builder sessions might contend for files or user interaction; multiple generated harnesses might share host resources. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No independent actor identifies and resolves particular cross-S1 interference through an adjustment returned to both units. - Decision owner: The server routes channels and reload events; users may direct sessions, but there is no explicit agent-owned inter-S1 coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-harness session directories, main/builder channel separation, spec manifest and deterministic reload hooks. - Closure path: Builder changes trigger refresh of the main harness; this is capability adaptation, not conflict recognition and mutual inter-agent coordination. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A separate builder channel and session router do not establish S2 merely because two agents exist. - Evidence: web channels, harness update skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A custom generated harness could introduce its own S2 in code outside this reviewed standard arrangement.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: No whole-arrangement discretionary supervisory control over current priorities, resources or commitments of multiple operating S1 units was identified. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current agent execution status, multi-step progress, missing keys, safety limits and session lifecycle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No manager uses a whole-system operational view to revise allocation or current commitments across agents. - Decision owner: User selects harness/model/policies; each model owns its own task; web server enforces channel/session management. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan, usage UI, model selector, RPC process lifecycle, static guard settings, session restart and status display. - Closure path: Plans and status feed back to the same task agent or user; no cross-unit supervisory current-control judgment returns to regulate the whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local planning, visibility and process control do not establish a managerial S3 function by themselves. - Evidence: core, web, plan. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: An operator may stop/steer a session, but this alone is not a parent-governed whole-system S3 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No independent complementary auditor challenges ordinary task-success claims and returns findings to a separate current-control owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect generated code, invalid specs, broken tools or misleading completion reports. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Validators and smoke/load checks mechanically test constraints but have no independent agent-owned audit judgment. - Decision owner: Forge&#39;s same task/build agent initiates checks; deterministic validators enforce configured formats, and the user may inspect output. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: forgevalidate, forgesmoke, view checker, type checks and generated-file edit detection. - Closure path: Validation errors return to the same builder agent for repair; no independent complementary audit/report-to-S3 route is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Testing an artifact, even in a separate subprocess, is ordinary build QA unless complementary independent audit ownership is established. - Evidence: Forge tools, update skill. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A developer can add independent review through custom code; not part of the first-party loaded mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Forge&#39;s model-based builder interprets prospective capability needs and develops changes to a target agent&#39;s future tools, skills, views, prompts and model settings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New domain requirements, tool gaps, user-identified failures and changing future tasks beyond the target agent&#39;s present tool repertoire. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a capability design, ask targeted clarifying questions, implement and iterate on candidate spec/code/skill changes for subsequent operation. - Decision owner: The Forge model agent, within user-defined scope and file/guard permissions; deterministic generation and smoke checks enforce its design. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scoped builderPrompt, harness-update, tool/view/skill-builder skills, forgegenerate, forgesmoke, reload and target-specific custom directories. - Closure path: User/future task distinction and existing harness inspection → model chooses a design → spec/code edits and validation → generator writes target package → web auto-reload or /reload exposes the new capability to later S1 turns. - Boundary reachability: The web server creates a dedicated Forge RPC builder for non-Forge harnesses and calls reloadHarness on settled modifications; Forge itself exposes build/update tools in normal model sessions. - External distinction: User-provided new workflow requirements and missing integrations, checked against target spec and tool inventory. - Future / prospective distinction: A new tool/skill/prompt is intended for subsequent tasks and new harness sessions, not simply to answer the current domain question. - Adaptation option generated: An agent-chosen modified harness.yaml, custom extension, skill and/or view, with validation and smoke-test plan. - Path back into current capability / S3: Generated assets and the main agent are reloaded; next user request can use new tools or knowledge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Although the human requests a capability, the model chooses the implementation and artifacts; the generator does not determine what should be built. - Evidence: builder wiring, update skill, tools, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Capability change is often user-initiated and may need approval/credentials; no autonomous long-horizon self-improvement mandate or guaranteed tested success is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No binding adjudication of ultimate harness identity/policy disputes by an autonomous or evidenced parent S5 path was found. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sandbox scope, protection of files, model/provider restrictions, acceptable tools and target identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Users select guards, models and targets in specs/settings; tool approval denies selected actions but no identity-level issue is escalated and ruled on through a first-party loop. - Decision owner: User/developer controls policy/configuration and can intervene per action; Forge may edit lower-level settings only within permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: harness.yaml guards, sandbox modes, protected paths, confirmation prompts, credentials and spec validation. - Closure path: Existing rules block or permit local actions; no legitimacy-governed S5 identity conversation and subsequent binding policy return is implemented. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A prompt/guard or proposed model change is not automatically ultimate-policy authority, and permission checks do not constitute S5. - Evidence: guards, sandbox, README. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: A human can rewrite the identity of a local generated harness, but this is not an established S5 governance channel.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9405fcfed091b78850008927c8c52e023dd1e4ba/assessments/pi/pi-forge.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9405fcfed091b78850008927c8c52e023dd1e4ba\">9405fcf &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T20:43:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Harvil1/codeAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CodeAgent (Harvil1)</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Harvil1/codeAgent/tree/e48780be52949dfcf17338025f6beab48ca01428\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e48780be52949dfcf17338025f6beab48ca01428\">e48780b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Select coding actions from changing repository observations and execute model-directed file/shell/tool work until task completion or bounded stop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User coding requirements, file contents, tool success/failure, budgets, permission denials. - Decisive decision or feedback right: AIAgent&#39;s model selects tools and follow-up actions after receiving tool results; no human per-step selection required in allowed mode. - Decision owner: The packaged AIAgent/model-led loop; external provider performs inference but does not supply a separate task-owning agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent iteration budget and stop handling, permission gates, modeltools dispatch, sessions/context, tool adapters. - Closure path: runconversation → model request → dispatchtoolcalls → tool result/history → next runconversation iteration or final answer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: AIAgent owns the decision/observation/continuation loop and can operate locally through the shipped CLI. A provider API alone would not produce this tool-control loop. - Boundary reachability: The supported cli.main / runinteractive instantiates AIAgent; the model-tool loop and tools are shipped first-party. - Evidence: agent/init.py; cli.py; modeltools.py; agent/llmclient.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Attenuate specific duplicate assignment and unauthorized task-state interference among separately running child worker S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running workers may attempt to claim/operate the same kanban task and corrupt shared task ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Deterministic conditional task claim arbitrates ownership; coordinator reports contested-task failure to the caller, and binding refuses writes to another worker&#39;s task. - Decision owner: First-party constructed TaskStore/TeamCoordinator ownership guard; discretionary rescope after a rejection belongs to the parent model, not to the file lock. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cross-process lock, conditional status/owner update, CODEAGENTKANBANTASK binding, tasktools ownership enforcement. - Closure path: Parent selects a worker/task ID → coordinator claims task atomically → conflicting claim returns failure to parent as tool result → later parent assignment can change; admitted worker&#39;s task mutations are bounded by binding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: This is narrower than generic bus/routing: it directly regulates competing ownership of a shared task between independent worker processes with a returned failure signal. It does not prove general repository file-conflict resolution. - Boundary reachability: teamspawn with taskid and task updates are first-party registered team/task tools used by the shipped model loop. - Distinct S1 units: first-party separately running agent/team/worker.py child coding model loops, each possibly assigned a TaskStore task in one parent-managed team. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two workers or successive assignments targeting one mutable task can contend for a single task owner, causing duplicate task-state writes/commitments. - Attenuating coordination relation: TeamCoordinator invokes atomic TaskStore.claim before worker process startup; claim rejects a task owned by another worker and a bound worker cannot update a different task. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a rejected teamspawn/claim returns failure as a tool result to the parent model which can choose a different task/worker on a later loop; task store owner and status are visible to subsequent workers. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the conditional owner transaction directly rejects a concrete inter-worker duplicate-claim disturbance and returns the rejection to the choosing parent; this is narrow task-ownership collision attenuation, not general file/worktree conflict handling. - Evidence: agent/taskstore.py; agent/team/coordinator.py; agent/team/taskbinding.py; tools/teamtool.py; tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate the live roster of child coding operations and their current task commitments at the main-agent team boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker availability, completion/failure, occupied task ownership, finite team capacity and conflicting active commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The parent model can inspect teammembers and task state and choose teamspawn, teamshutdown and task assignments during an active session. - Decision owner: The main AIAgent using shipped team/task tools; TeamCoordinator enforces member caps and process transitions but does not choose the plan. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Registry with member status/role, teammembers, task tools, spawn/shutdown, message bus, worker status updates. - Closure path: Live roster/task results enter the model&#39;s next turn → model chooses worker/task intervention → coordinator changes running processes / task owner → returned status informs subsequent turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Distinct from mere process manager: a model-operable whole-current team roster and intervention tools are wired into the same first-party feedback loop. This is a structural capability claim, not a measured autonomous scheduling benchmark. - Boundary reachability: Team tools are registered with the shipped agent&#39;s callable toolset, not a developer CI script or external scheduler. - Whole-system current view: teammembers returns status/role/PID of all first-party team workers, and the task store exposes assigned current work and ownership to the main agent. - Current-control decision scope: main model can choose which worker/task to spawn, inspect current membership, shut down running workers, and revise current task commitments; deterministic team size/depth ceilings enforce but do not choose those decisions. - Evidence: tools/teamtool.py; agent/team/coordinator.py; agent/team/worker.py; agent/init.py; tools/tasktools.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Provide sufficiently independent operational reality inspection with corrective return to the whole-current S3 controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Child reports may be inaccurate, tool outputs incomplete, and operator state may drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately established organization-wide complementary audit judgment with corrective S3 return is demonstrated. - Decision owner: Child-output checks and optional aux-LLM review are supporting validators; a distinct S3 audit owner is not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Child-claim filesystem/TaskStore check, appendwarning, optional handoffreviewenabled, trace logs. - Closure path: Claim mismatch warnings can reach parent context, but the verifier is scoped to a single delegation and does not own a complementary assessment of current system regulation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cross-check of declared files/tasks is real yet narrowly local; source does not demonstrate an independent audit of S3 operations or an audit-based current-control corrective obligation. - Evidence: agent/team/hallucinationcheck.py; tools/delegatechild.py; agent/trace.py; config.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Monitor the external future environment and choose/provide prospective organizational adaptation to the current coding system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New external coding environments, future model/tool needs, shifts in operating strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party whole-system external/prospective adaptive decision right is demonstrated. - Decision owner: Developer/user controls extensions and learning enablement; heuristic skill evolution operates on past in-session trace patterns. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Disabled-by-default skilllearning observer/evolver, skill storage, memory/compaction, workflow and hooks. - Closure path: When enabled, repeated past tool patterns can become local skill files; the source does not close external-future sensing → strategic selection → changed operational capacity as an S4 function. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An auto-generated habit from task traces is retrospective task improvement; the user-enabled feature by itself does not establish environment-facing prospective S4 governance. - Evidence: agent/skilllearning/turnobserver.py; agent/skilllearning/evolver.py; agent/skilllearning/observer.py; config.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve legitimacy, ultimate identity and binding mission/policy choices of the harness organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting user goals, safety settings, credentials and authorization limits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Operators set higher-level objective/configuration; ordinary command approvals and system prompt constraints do not resolve organization-wide identity disputes. - Decision owner: Operator/developer owns settings, permissions and scope outside assessed autonomy; shipped code enforces rather than closes ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission subsystem, command/secret guards, config settings and model tool allowlists. - Closure path: Approvals or denials affect local tool calls; no supported first-party policy-identity escalation and authoritative returned decision loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Local tool-control policy and user acceptance are not themselves an operational S5 mission/policy closure. - Evidence: agent/permission.py; config.py; agent/init.py; tools/teamtool.py. - Basis: explicit shipped control paths and structural source-first reconstruction at the frozen revision. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: This is static source evidence, not a live benchmark; scope is limited to first-party packaged operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8b7f3f939013ab310a253b2b144d5f8e2654644a/assessments/codeagent-harvil1.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8b7f3f939013ab310a253b2b144d5f8e2654644a\">8b7f3f9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T17:33:27+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AmbientCrypto/ambient-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ambient CLI</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AmbientCrypto/ambient-cli/tree/46dfd8aada3eb73f4446b6b2e3c34c6b3c7a68b9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 46dfd8aada3eb73f4446b6b2e3c34c6b3c7a68b9\">46dfd8a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: make coding and repository investigation decisions from user goals, real workspace state and returned tools/results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: source unknowns, code errors, tool failures, user changes, model uncertainty, tests/diagnostics and delegated work outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select file/tool actions, revise edits, launch bounded children and respond to actual observations. - Decision owner: the main Ambient model-backed Agent, plus separately model-driven child agents within assigned read-only or writable mandates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: compiled tool registry, permission/risk classifier, memory and context, model-controller lane, executor, session persistence, abort/budget limits and human confirmation. - Closure path: task/prompt + repository context → model-selected tool or delegation → first-party file/shell/tool execution → observed result/report → model revisions or completion. - Boundary reachability: normal ambient CLI wires first-party Agent with project tools and the advertised subagent tool; the child tool invokes actual new Agent(...).run(...) with scoped context and returns observed effects to the same parent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing code edits and investigative responses needs model discretion; deterministic permissions and runtime cannot perform these decisions alone. - Evidence: Agent runtime, built-in child tool, tool registry. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no live model-effectiveness claim; read-only/oracle and builder tools have distinct capabilities and approval paths.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: stabilize simultaneous independently acting worker S1 units competing for the operator&#39;s single question/approval resolution channel. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two builder children asking approval concurrently would each overwrite the same parent prompt resolver or strand a promise; independent child questions similarly collide at the user interaction endpoint. - Distinct S1 units: child Agent.run instances within the same runSubagents wave, each with separate task, model context, child ID, tool decisions and permission questions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous calls to the shared user-facing approval/question resolver can clobber another worker&#39;s outstanding request and prevent a correct answer reaching its author. - Attenuating coordination relation: a first-party per-wave approveChain queues approvals and askChain queues questions, while non-asking children continue operating in parallel. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each queued approval/question resolves against its original child tool action, allowing that child to execute or adapt/retry; the result is then included in its normal model/tool loop. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: source directly names and prevents a concrete mutual-interference failure among concurrent children over one scarce interactive decision slot, returning each decision to the correct continuing worker. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose how concurrent child approvals are serialized and resumed; shipped order is deterministic, authored by the developer, not renegotiated by another autonomous agent. - Decision owner: developer-provided first-party approval queue/coordination policy. The main model can choose which tasks to launch but does not autonomously decide the shared-resolver contention policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate child contexts and task IDs, promise chains, bounded parallel wave workers and role-limited permissions. - Closure path: concurrent child tool effects request approval → serialized resolver queue → original human decision delivered to each child → child executes/denies and continues with the observed result. - Boundary reachability: makeSubagentTool injects runSubagents into normal CLI tools, and its child Approver/AskPort are replaced with the shipped gated queues; this is not a test-only lock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model agents own their coding actions, but changing the S2 exclusion/ordering rule would require runtime modification or developer composition rather than the model&#39;s organizational discretion. - Evidence: runSubagents approval/question chains, CLI subagent instantiation, question/approval surface. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: attenuates question/approval-slot contention; does not claim general workspace conflict resolution or model negotiation among builders.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no independently established whole-current resource/commitment manager with authority to revise a portfolio of S1 commitments beyond composing a current delegated coding wave. - Disturbance / variety regulated: background child progress, shared user approvals and per-role deadlines are managed for one requested task, not a durable independently accountable multi-mission portfolio. - Whole-system current view: the TUI sees child started/finished progress, and the main model receives child summaries; it does not receive a separate standing whole-current performance/commitment ledger spanning independent operations. - Current-control decision scope: the main model selects wave jobs/roles and integrates reports; the user can interrupt or request early wrap-up. Four-way concurrency and per-child time/turn caps are fixed runtime limits, not model-owned global resource bargaining. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate cross-unit current-control decision evidenced beyond initial delegation, ordinary task integration and human interruption. - Decision owner: model for operational decomposition; human and fixed runtime for approval/limits; no independently demonstrated S3 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runSubagents wave statuses, task records, completion summaries, fixed concurrency caps, soft/hard deadlines and parent cancellation. - Closure path: child task start → aggregate result into main coding task exists; independent current S1 commitment state → whole-system intervention → revised continuing child commitments is not wired as a separate control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing tasks at one delegation point does not establish whole-current S3; task overview is not a discretionary organizational control loop without returned reallocations. - Evidence: child wave and caps, CLI subagent tool, runtime Agent. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a team-shaped task can be sizeable; a different broader organization integrating ongoing workspace management might support S3, but no such first-party control return is credited here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: provide an optional independent second-opinion read of actual repository artifacts, distinct from the main coding Agent&#39;s own narrative and ordinary post-edit checks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unobserved design/correctness mistakes or optimistic self-reporting after code changes may escape the author&#39;s tool/test account. - Claim being audited: that proposed or completed project code changes are sound for the requested design/task. - Ordinary reporting path: main model tools, builder output and current diagnostics/verify scripts return into its usual coding context. - Complementary access path: the built-in subagent tool exposes explicit oracle role for review; a separate model-backed child session with read-only file/search tools inspects source directly and reports independent findings. Auto routing selects reviewer-oriented fleet models (without guaranteeing distinct model identity). - Independence boundary: separate Agent instance/session, own prompt and context and enforced read-only tool set, rather than a rephrased main-agent summary; user-authored extra reviewer scripts or external platform evaluators are not credited. - Who acts on findings: the parent coding Agent, which receives and may act on the oracle&#39;s review summary; oracle cannot mutate files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what shortcomings the directly observed code has, using model judgment in the separate review mandate. - Decision owner: the first-party instantiated oracle child model for semantic review, opt-in when the main Agent selects a review delegation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-based tool registry, separate child session/history, RoutedRole=reviewer, summary cap, event/progress stream and parent result injection. - Closure path: existing code → separate read-only oracle&#39;s direct file inspection → reviewer-model assessment → returned child report → parent model can revise coding actions. - Boundary reachability: oracle is one of three advertised shipped subagent roles, is dispatched through actual new Agent(...).run, and read-only permissions are enforced in the child registry; no custom reviewer plugin is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model chooses semantic review findings; the deterministic read-only rule and output cap enforce isolation but do not make the audit judgment. - Evidence: role schema/registry, actual child reviewer model runner, reviewer role fleet route, system delegation instruction. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional, not mandatory; no live demonstration or empirical reviewer effectiveness. The oracle may resolve to the same underlying model family as its parent; independence here is context and direct read-only evidence, not guaranteed model-provider diversity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: re-evaluate alternative external model capabilities from a changing live inference fleet and accumulated real-request performance before adopting a more suitable model for subsequent coding runs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: models become cold/unavailable, new candidates appear with differing tool/reasoning/context capability, and experience reveals persistent differences in request success and latency. - External distinction: the current Ambient live catalogue exposes per-model readiness and advertised capabilities, while local capability records persist observed provider outcomes across earlier requests. - Future / prospective distinction: a next run/phase may benefit from a different available model; a score ranks alternative candidates from current fleet plus historical success/latency instead of hard-coding one model identity. - Adaptation option generated: select the highest-scored ready, tool-capable candidate; role-specific planner/executor/reviewer/compactor selectors further specialize the next model choice. - Path back into current capability / S3: when requestedModel=auto or a role is routed, Agent.resolveModel applies the selected model and lane to the next model call, and subsequent outcomes update stored performance evidence for future decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which external model-capability option replaces/serves the next coding model run based on reported and learned evidence. - Decision owner: developer-specified deterministic scoring and role-selection heuristics; no separately agent-owned S4 strategy actor is present. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ambient live catalogue, CapabilityStore, per-model sampled success/latency and tool-call evidence, model-resolution events, fallbacks and context budgeting. - Closure path: external fleet states + past request experience → bounded candidate scoring → selected role/default model applied for the next Agent request → outcomes recorded for later re-selection. - Boundary reachability: CLI injects makeCapabilityPort; runtime Agent calls resolveRequestedModel with its persisted stats and pickForRole on auto routed work, not merely a developer roadmap or bare library helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective option and adaptation return are implemented, but the decisive scoring policy is fixed TypeScript; an independent autonomous strategy selector/replanner would require additional composition. - Evidence: live-catalog scoring, future-model resolution and application, persisted learning port, run outcome capture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: S4=C is restricted to adaptive model capability selection for later operations, not autonomous changes to the product&#39;s mission, tools, source or long-range business strategy. Role choice is heuristic, not validated as optimal, and historical outcomes need sufficient samples.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve and legitimately revise a session-long ultimate coding objective when new evidence makes its north-star purpose obsolete or inconsistent with subordinate plans. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool-level plans or newly revealed constraints might conflict with the durable user-chosen mission; the agent must not silently move its own identity-level objective. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whether to keep or replace the user-defined --goal//goal north star that explicitly outranks intermediate plans and governs every subsequent run in this session. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: legitimate human session owner in supported interactive mode. The model can propose a revision but cannot commit an identity-level change; no autonomous S5 mode claimed. - Return-to-operation path: model proposegoalupdate → interactive question including reason and proposed purpose → user confirms/edits or declines → only approval emits durable goal.set → pinned system-prompt goal and future turns follow the accepted identity. The explicit user /goal command also changes the purpose directly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept, reject or rewrite the session mission itself, rather than approve one routine shell/edit action. - Decision owner: human operator/session owner; a model-generated proposal is subordinate and non-binding. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ctx.ask confirmation UI, reducer goal.set, session JSONL persistence, compaction goal anchor, next-turn system prompt and child goal inheritance. - Closure path: conflict with ultimate session purpose → agent proposal → legitimate human edit/confirmation → goal.set event → persistent next-turn prompt/children constrained by new user-chosen goal. - Boundary reachability: proposegoalupdate is registered as a first-party built-in tool and interactive TUI binds the approval to the live session with durable event return. In headless/no-user mode the proposal explicitly changes nothing, so S5=P is claimed only for supported interactive mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the human owner leaves the proposal without binding effect; the model cannot silently change the goal, so final identity decision belongs to parent. - Evidence: first-party goal proposal/approval, goal outranks plans, goal events in interactive session, goal restore. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is S5 at the coding session&#39;s purpose recursion only, not Ambient corporate/OSS identity governance; no session goal means the option remains dormant, and headless operation has no interactive parent return.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3463c6e3c2d7a4f339df2dd725fd2bfd2fb4f006/assessments/am/ambient-cli.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3463c6e3c2d7a4f339df2dd725fd2bfd2fb4f006\">3463c6e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T18:41:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GustavoGutierrez/alisio\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Alisio</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/GustavoGutierrez/alisio/tree/4a3c9c21b9e005f7bf8ebcd256bee5de60dc2e48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4a3c9c21b9e005f7bf8ebcd256bee5de60dc2e48\">4a3c9c2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute user-requested coding operations in the repository, including independent bounded child tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar code, errors, shell results, changing task requirements, permissions, provider output and child-task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model-backed primary and child actors select tools/arguments and adapt subsequent actions to tool output and instructions. - Decision owner: primary Alisio model agent and distinct subordinate model sessions in supported delegation mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider/session bindings, tool registry, permissions, active-agent selection, child session creation, depth/turn limits and tool-result persistence. - Closure path: model decides tool or child task → runtime executes or denies → tool output/child result enters that actor&#39;s or parent&#39;s conversation → agent selects further work. - Boundary reachability: CLI ships the core runner and enables the subagents plugin by default; its built-in general/explore/plan definitions instantiate separate child model sessions via registered tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses work and responds to results; runner and permissions execute/constrain but do not preselect individual code edits. - Evidence: runner.ts, index.ts, manager.ts. - Basis: structural source and first-party operating documentation; native deterministic subagent tests. - Confidence: high for supported operating path. - Caveats: no connected live LLM was run; native tests corroborate constructor and control behavior, not model quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: dampen interference among independently deciding child coding S1s working under one primary agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent write-capable children may overwrite one another&#39;s changes or generate conflicting repository modifications. - Distinct S1 units: two or more child model sessions with separate conversations and scoped tool rights, scheduled through the built-in task tool. - Inter-S1 disturbance: startTask explicitly detects at least two overlapping write-capable children and activates a collision-specific mitigation path; read-only work does not require writer isolation. - Attenuating coordination relation: the primary model selects child roles, bounds instructions and work decomposition and can adjust running children via sendmessage; the built-in manager then serializes writers or uses isolated git worktrees under the configured/operator-chosen policy. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: assigned child instructions and write boundaries govern the work they undertake; queued/finished results and worktree diffstats return to the parent agent, which can revise later delegation or message a child. Worktree changes require explicit integration before affecting the shared tree. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the separate mitigation is triggered by an implemented concurrent write collision class, and changes the execution location/ordering of precisely those S1s; it is not a generic task queue or chat transport alone. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model decides which independent child work to run and its task-specific coordination boundaries, and can revise a child after result feedback; an interactive human separately chooses worktree/serial/shared collision policy when prompted. - Decision owner: model-backed primary actor for task-specific coordination; interactive operator for the write-safety policy choice; deterministic SubagentManager for enforcement only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: 40 ms overlapping-writer detection, config parallelWrites, terminal selector, serial mutex, git-worktree setup, child results/diffstat, taskstatus and sendmessage. - Closure path: primary selects concurrent child roles/prompts → manager detects write contention and enforces selected isolation/serialization → child S1s work within resulting constraints → their results and branch changes return → primary revises future coordinated delegation. - Boundary reachability: default-enabled plugin registers the agent-owned task/sendmessage tools and ships built-in independently executing children plus a write-specific first-party safeguard. Headless runs use serial writes without requiring interactive selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removal of the primary actor leaves only enforcement of already selected collision policies, not task-specific decisions about which child work should coexist or how results should alter subsequent child instructions. The operator&#39;s independent choice of safety mode is not falsely attributed to the model. - Evidence: manager.ts, index.ts, docs/subagents.md, tests/subagents.test.ts. - Basis: explicit interference guard plus structural independent child-agent decision/feedback path. - Confidence: medium-high: S2 is bounded to one primary agent&#39;s cooperating child set, not a globally autonomous distributed coordinator. - Caveats: the default interactive write-safety mode is chosen by the human rather than the model; agent autonomy lies in task-specific child coordination and feedback, with code enforcing write isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-current system resource/commitment-control authority evidenced beyond parent task delegation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child task capacity, deadlines and token limits exist, but the shipped scheduling layer enforces configured caps instead of making a whole-system discretionary commitment decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent can launch/retry/message individual tasks; static runtime admits, queues, bounds or cancels sessions. - Decision owner: no evidenced owner of whole-current resource bargaining, organizational priority negotiation or cross-unit accountability at the assessed recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-parent/global concurrency caps, queue, session tree, token budgets, TUI cancel/merge commands and taskstatus. - Closure path: child work/reports reach parent and optional operator commands alter individual child execution; a separate whole-system resource/priority decision-return path is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: choosing an agent for a subtask and observing its progress is ordinary decomposition; structural limits do not give their enforcer discretion over present organization-wide commitments. - Evidence: manager.ts, config.ts, docs/subagents.md. - Basis: bounded first-party implementation inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a custom primary prompt or outside orchestrator could exercise broader control, but it is not supplied by this standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent operational-reality audit loop is wired to corrective control in the default product. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code errors can be tested or reviewed, but no complementary evidence-based challenge of normal child reports is installed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a generic user-authored reviewer may read a diff, and output of ordinary tests returns to the producing agent; neither supplies first-party independent audit ownership. - Decision owner: no built-in S3 audit actor established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child role definition loader, read-only permissions, Git tools, testing and result wrappers warning that child reports are not authoritative. - Closure path: ordinary tool/child results return to the primary operational path; no separately wired reviewer observation → audit judgment → corrective whole-control return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a sample reviewer definition demonstrates composability but is not standard shipped audit closure or independent sampling of operational artifacts. - Evidence: definitions.ts, manager.ts, docs/subagents.md. - Basis: inspection of default agent catalog, delegation, returned results and optional reviewer example. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream users can configure reviewers, but the assessed standard mode does not prescribe independent audit inputs, authority and returned corrective action.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed external/future-oriented capability adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated session findings and environment information may be recorded, but prospective product redesign is not automatically selected or returned into the active harness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active model can plan its current task, while user/config controls memory/provider/agents; neither decides an S4 future capability change from environmental scanning. - Decision owner: no prospective organizational adaptation authority identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: @alisio/plugin-memory SQLite search and compaction, provider switches, skills, customizable agents, model planning. - Closure path: stored notes and retrieved context inform later coding requests; no environment horizon → adaptation options → selected durable capability change → current-control return is wired. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic memory and user-configured plugins facilitate S1 decisions, but do not establish an S4-specific option-selection/implementation owner. - Evidence: plugin-memory/src/index.ts, plugin-memory/src/store.ts, builtin.ts. - Basis: bounded memory/config/provider and runtime inspection. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a user&#39;s coding task may build future-facing software, but that is still S1 delivery without the organizational S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no legitimate ultimate identity/policy adjudication with return to the coding organization is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: untrusted shell/files and unsafe parallel edits trigger permissions/coordination choices, not institutional purpose conflicts between current and future viability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator grants or refuses tool requests and chooses write-isolation posture; fixed config narrows sessions. - Decision owner: operator owns local permissions but no function-specific ultimate organizational S5 decision circuit is installed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trust settings, tool permission checks, child inheritance, bounded task scopes and interactive selection. - Closure path: action approval/denial alters one operation; no identity-level issue reaches a legitimate parent who decides ultimate policy and returns that decision as a governing rule to subsequent organization-wide operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a permission gate, project config or human merge command does not create ultimate identity/ethos authority. - Evidence: runner.ts, manager.ts, docs/subagents.md. - Basis: reviewed supported permissions, configuration and operator paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: product maintainers govern releases at an adjacent recursion, not the assessed running session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8e1f5f4555abcb899e02683ff0b8d7c2bbc74eed/assessments/al/alisio.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8e1f5f4555abcb899e02683ff0b8d7c2bbc74eed\">8e1f5f4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T22:13:35Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nichonauta/MinAgent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MinAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Nichonauta/MinAgent/tree/02880e3c981ba57cd95d2266d6c0ea07b1abe44c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02880e3c981ba57cd95d2266d6c0ea07b1abe44c\">02880e3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a coding request into repository/process work or a task answer through model-selected actions with returned environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository/file state, implementation alternatives, tool and process results, endpoint uncertainty, context pressure, workspace guidance and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding/tool action and revise subsequent choices from returned environment evidence. - Decision owner: the configured model actor inside the first-party MinAgent conversation loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool dispatcher, workspace confinement and write verification, terminal/MCP approval gates, context compaction, token/tool-round limits, skills/MCP adapters and streaming client. - Closure path: user objective/current context → model chooses tool call or answer → first-party MinAgent executes the permitted action → actual result is appended as a tool message → the same model receives that result and chooses another action or final response. - Boundary reachability: the documented minagent/node src/minagent.mjs entrypoint directly instantiates this shipped loop; built-in file tools and optional terminal/skills/MCP tools are presented through that same standard runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the model actor is removed while tool schemas, workspace protections, limits and approvals remain, the runtime can enforce preselected constraints but does not choose task-specific coding actions. The substantive operational discretion therefore belongs to the model actor. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs; src/workspace.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference occurs through an external compatible endpoint, but first-party MinAgent closes its decisions through the workspace/tool feedback loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material inter-S1 disturbance-attenuation function is supplied at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: no interaction-generated conflict or oscillation among multiple operational S1 units is established because the standard MinAgent composition exposes one primary operational agent loop. - Distinct S1 units: not established. Built-in tools, shell commands, MCP servers and skills are capabilities/dependencies used by the one operating agent rather than first-party autonomous operational units with their own durable local outcome/autonomy at this recursion. - Inter-S1 disturbance: not established. - Attenuating coordination relation: not established. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: tool results feed the same S1&#39;s next decision, not a coordination outcome among distinct S1s. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: it is not; the inspected paths are tool invocation, request sequencing and context transport inside one operational loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: sequential tool-round loop, tool-call count limits, MCP/skill dispatch and process/file execution. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model owns one S1&#39;s next action, but there is no separate coordination function over multiple S1s to own. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream users can connect external multi-agent MCP systems, but adjacent external compositions do not create first-party S2 for MinAgent itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-system current-control function is established above the single operating MinAgent S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: context pressure, tool limits, terminal permissions and workspace boundaries are regulated locally, but there is no population-level current resource/commitment control on behalf of a larger operational whole. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: token/context budgets, automatic compaction, tool-round limits, terminal/MCP approval, process cleanup and workspace safety checks. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Whole-system current view: MinAgent can estimate its own context, refresh its own workspace snapshot and track its own conversation/tool state; this is the current state of one S1 task, not a whole-system view over several autonomous operational commitments. - Current-control decision scope: deterministic limits and user approvals constrain one session. No separate actor allocates resources, priorities, commitments or interventions across autonomous S1 units. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model regulates its coding actions as S1, while deterministic runtime mechanisms regulate service/context bounds. Neither establishes a distinct S3 function at the chosen recursion. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: current-state visibility is real but remains local operational state rather than metasystemic whole-current control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit loop with sufficiently independent access and returned corrective control is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workspace operations use strong inline validation, but the standard runtime does not supply a separate complementary observer that can challenge ordinary S1 claims through materially different access. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic workspace operations, post-write reread/byte comparison, path identity checks, ordinary tool results and tests outside the assessed runtime. - Closure path: not applicable; inline execution validation returns the result of the same operation, not an independent audit finding into organizational control. - Claim being audited: no distinct operational claim is assigned to a complementary auditor. - Ordinary reporting path: built-in tool execution returns actual file/process results as tool messages to the operating model. - Complementary access path: not established. Post-write verification rereads the just-written target as part of the same first-party tool operation; it does not constitute alternative, sporadic or organizationally independent access beyond the normal production path. - Independence boundary: none qualifying in the standard distribution. External MCP services or developer tests are adjacent unless specifically composed into the runtime and do not establish first-party audit ownership here. - Who acts on findings: ordinary tool errors/results return to the operating model as S1 feedback; there is no distinct S3 finding/return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model can react to tool failures, while deterministic file validation supports safe execution. No separate agent owns a complementary audit judgment. - Evidence: README.md; src/workspace.mjs; src/minagent.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: strong verification is credited as operational safety evidence, not promoted to S3 without complementary independence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: model the current project/workspace environment and turn those distinctions into durable project-specific guidance that changes later MinAgent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variation in repository architecture, important directories, build/test commands, conventions, checks and stale or missing project guidance that would otherwise force later coding turns to rediscover project-specific operating knowledge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what project distinctions matter and what durable architecture/command/convention/check guidance should be written into the root AGENTS.md for subsequent operation. - Decision owner: the configured model actor invoked by MinAgent&#39;s first-party /init path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: deterministic project-file candidate scoring/selection, workspace inventory, bounded file excerpts, secret redaction, atomic workspace write, workspace refresh and automatic AGENTS.md reload before model requests. - Closure path: /init invocation → first-party inventory and selected project evidence → model develops project-specific future operating guidance → MinAgent writes/updates root AGENTS.md → workspace refresh reloads that guidance → subsequent S1 model requests include it in the system prompt and can operate differently under the returned adaptation. - Boundary reachability: /init [focus] is a documented built-in command in the standard interactive runtime; its implementation calls collectProjectEssentials, invokes the configured model, writes AGENTS.md, and refreshes the same first-party workspace context used by later requests. - External distinction: repository/workspace evidence outside the harness itself, including project architecture, important files, commands, conventions and checks selected from the current project. - Future / prospective distinction: the generated guidance explicitly captures project knowledge intended to shape later coding work rather than merely answer the current request; stale existing guidance is to be corrected for subsequent use. - Adaptation option generated: the model selects and synthesizes the complete project-specific AGENTS.md content from the observed evidence, including which architecture facts, commands, conventions and checks should become durable guidance. - Path back into current capability / S3: MinAgent persists the selected adaptation as root AGENTS.md; refreshWorkspaceSnapshot loads it, and workspace inventory reloads AGENTS.md before every later model request into the system prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user triggers /init and may optionally supply focus, but deterministic selection/writing machinery does not determine the substantive project guidance. If the model actor is removed, the runtime still has an inventory and writer but no autonomous decision over what durable adaptation to synthesize from project evidence. The decisive adaptation judgment is therefore model-owned. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs; src/init-project.mjs; src/workspace.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: /init is user-invoked rather than continuously scheduled, but invocation is not the decisive adaptation judgment. No distinct parent-governed S4 mode is claimed merely because a human can invoke or manually edit project guidance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy authority loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompts, AGENTS.md, tool/workspace boundaries, terminal mode and MCP approval constrain operation, but the strongest policy premises are externally configured or belong to ordinary task/tool governance rather than runtime identity-level adjudication. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: user/operator and static first-party configuration for the strongest candidate policy constraints; the model owns S1 task decisions and S4 project-guidance synthesis, not ultimate organizational identity/policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: base system prompt, AGENTS.md, workspace boundary, TERMINALMODE, MCP approval, configuration validation, tool-call/round limits and skills/MCP enablement flags. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding; constraints are loaded/enforced or per-call approval is requested, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue is adjudicated by a legitimate runtime ultimate authority and returned as a revised governing premise. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: no first-party standard path frames a genuine MinAgent identity or ultimate-policy dispute for runtime resolution. /init adapts project-operating guidance and therefore maps to S4, not S5. - Ultimate authority: user/operator and shipped/configured constraints for tool permissions and runtime setup; no autonomous ultimate-policy actor is established. - Return-to-operation path: configured modes and approvals immediately affect ordinary tool execution, and generated AGENTS.md affects later work, but these paths do not carry an identity/ultimate-policy decision. The latter is already credited to project adaptation under S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model cannot autonomously redefine the runtime&#39;s ultimate workspace/approval/configuration premises through a first-party identity-governance loop. Removing the human/config sources leaves enforcement machinery but not an S5 decision process. - Evidence: README.md; src/minagent.mjs; src/config.mjs; src/workspace.mjs. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: durable project guidance is not treated as identity merely because it is loaded into the system prompt; its documented purpose is project-specific future operating knowledge.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/57741270a63fffc0db0655d9ba05455aa737c7e7/assessments/minagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 57741270a63fffc0db0655d9ba05455aa737c7e7\">5774127 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-28T16:21:02Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/polyphemus-ai/release-rehearsal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Polyphemus</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-28\">2026-09-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/polyphemus-ai/release-rehearsal/tree/02020f3bc2829c350fd726273a7721254068925f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 02020f3bc2829c350fd726273a7721254068925f\">02020f3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform substantive project work through model-driven reasoning, tool use and iterative response to environment/tool feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguous task requests, changing project/repository state, tool results and failures, external service responses, model uncertainty, provider/tool differences and local implementation/research variety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next task-specific reasoning/tool action and revise later actions from returned observations in order to complete the assigned outcome. - Decision owner: the model-driven agent actor executed through the first-party Polyphemus session/workflow runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runTurn, provider adapters, model routing/fallback, tool registry, approvals, redaction, isolation workers, session persistence, skills/persona/project context and connection grants. - Closure path: user/workflow outcome + project/agent context → model actor selects an action/tool call → Polyphemus executes it within the allowed boundary → tool/environment result returns to the model → subsequent model action changes until the outcome/turn closes. - Boundary reachability: ordinary polyphemus sessions and workflow agent nodes directly invoke the shipped first-party loop and tools; no adjacent dogfood or contributor actor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven actor while keeping routing, tools, isolation and persistence leaves execution machinery but removes the task-specific discretionary action choice. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/loop.ts; packages/core/src/projects.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model/provider internals and vendor CLI internals remain external dependencies. A credits the model actor reached through the first-party supported runtime, not organizational functions internal to those providers.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate a concrete interference mode created when distinct S1 agent runtimes work concurrently in one shared thread while preserving each runtime&#39;s coherent model-visible history. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrently working agents interleave messages in the shared thread; another agent&#39;s messages can appear between a tool call and its tool result, and a runtime can otherwise continue from a private history that omits intervening peer contributions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine the safe model-visible ordering/synchronization of shared-thread messages so each runtime sees its tool call/result pairs coherently while still incorporating peer contributions. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic Polyphemus runtime policy (stitchHistory plus shared-thread resynchronization); no autonomous agent owns the coordination semantics. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-agent aside runtimes, addressed-agent dispatch, shared thread store, per-agent stop state, held-message queue, sequence tracking and runtime/session persistence. - Closure path: concurrent S1 runtimes append interleaved shared-thread events → Polyphemus reconstructs model-valid history and refreshes the shared thread before subsequent turns → each affected agent receives the repaired/current conversation → later S1 behavior proceeds from coordinated state rather than the destructive interleaving/stale copy. - Boundary reachability: concurrent addressed agents and history stitching are shipped daemon/core behavior documented and implemented at the pinned revision; adopters do not have to invent a separate coordination protocol. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing either model actor does not remove the deterministic coordination policy; the same first-party runtime rule decides how interleaved shared history is repaired. The S2-specific path is therefore constructor/runtime-owned C, not agent-owned A. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently addressed model-driven agent runtimes working concurrently in the same Polyphemus thread. - Inter-S1 disturbance: peer output interleaves with another agent&#39;s tool call/result sequence and can leave a model-facing history invalid or stale with respect to the shared conversation. - Attenuating coordination relation: stitchHistory moves tool results next to their calls, synthesizes a stopped-turn result where needed and preserves intervening peer material after the repaired pair; the multi-agent runtime also re-synchronizes each agent with shared thread state before later work. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the repaired/current history is what the next model invocation receives, so subsequent reasoning/actions are conditioned on coordinated peer state rather than corrupted or permanently skipped context. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicitly documented concurrency-induced model-history corruption/staleness mode and a shipped attenuation transform that changes later sibling-agent input. Addressing, queueing and shared storage alone are not used as the S2 witness. - Evidence: docs/design/parallel-agents.md; packages/core/src/history.ts; packages/daemon/src/server.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the held-message queue and agent addressing are generic routing/sequencing mechanisms by themselves. The positive S2 finding is narrower: concurrent shared-thread history interference plus the specific repair/resynchronization path.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S3 function is established at the assessed project/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-run failures, step state, budgets, provider exhaustion, approvals and concurrent job status are regulated locally, but the reviewed boundary does not establish a separate whole-project current regulator with authority over the portfolio of S1 resources/commitments/priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying whole-system current-control decision right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: durable run store, node state machine, retries, hard attempt/loop/budget limits, one-at-a-time workflow keys, provider fallback/capacity readings, session stop controls, scheduler and human gates. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model nodes make local operational decisions, while the workflow engine deterministically advances fixed graphs and enforces preselected limits. Ordinary operator gates approve specific actions. Neither path demonstrates a separate agent-owned or constructor-specific whole-system current regulator at the selected recursion. - Evidence: docs/design/workflows.md; packages/core/src/workflows/define.ts; packages/daemon/src/runs.ts; packages/core/src/routing.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: at a narrower workflow-run recursion the deterministic engine strongly regulates execution, and a future portfolio-level scheduler/manager could change this assessment. The current conclusion is specific to the declared project/deployment recursion and Profile threshold.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge a produced software change using access and judgment distinct from the builder&#39;s ordinary production path, then return findings into correction before merge. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects, missing requirements, risky changes or false confidence that can survive the producing agent&#39;s own reasoning and objective checks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the exact candidate and decide whether to approve it or request changes based on direct repository/diff/test evidence. - Decision owner: the read-only model-driven Reviewer agent instantiated by the first-party ship-issue workflow; the workflow preferentially selects a different model vendor from the producing agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh node session; independentOf provider selection; read-only mode; exact-head push/check linkage; typed review artifact; deterministic verdict parsing; GitHub Reviewer identity; exact-head posted review; merge guard. - Closure path: builder candidate + objective checks at exact head → separate reviewer agent directly inspects diff/files and runs relevant checks → reviewer returns approve or requestchanges with concerns → deterministic workflow either passes the approval check or loops findings back to the work node → repaired candidate is rechecked/re-reviewed before merge. - Boundary reachability: ship-issue is a built-in shipped workflow; its review node is explicitly readOnly and independentOf the producing node, and the run engine implements other-vendor reviewer selection in ordinary workflow execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment about defects/adequacy comes from the separate reviewer model actor. Exact-head validation, read-only restrictions and merge gating enforce that judgment but do not decide the review verdict. - Claim being audited: that the candidate change at the exact pushed head satisfies the requested issue/plan and is safe/complete enough to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: the builder&#39;s submitted change report plus objective build/typecheck/lint/test/preview checks and workflow artifacts. - Complementary access path: a separate read-only reviewer session reads the actual diff/files and runs its own relevant checks against the candidate; it is not limited to the builder&#39;s self-report. - Independence boundary: the review node is declared independentOf the producing work node; the run engine attempts to choose a ready model from another vendor, and the bundled Reviewer is instructed not to edit/merge the candidate it audits. - Who acts on findings: the workflow&#39;s approval check and repair loop return requestchanges concerns to the producing work node; subsequent push/check/review repeats on the corrected head. Merge additionally verifies an approval by an identity other than author/merger at the exact head. - Evidence: docs/design/workflows.md; packages/core/src/workflows/ship.ts; packages/core/src/workflows/define.ts; packages/daemon/src/runs.ts; packages/core/templates/agents/reviewer/instructions.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: when no ready model from another vendor exists, the run engine can fall back to the same vendor and records that loss of vendor diversity. The positive state relies on the standard supported mode in which independent reviewer selection is available and on role/session/read-only separation plus direct artifact access, not on vendor diversity alone.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established at the assessed project/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: provider capacity, project history, current external events and scheduled triggers are observed, but the standard distribution does not convert a model of changing external/future conditions into adaptation options that return through S3 to change present organizational capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying prospective adaptation judgment/feedback right is established. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider usage forecasting, fallback routing, project memory/notes/handoff, orientation, routines/triggers, web/external connections and editable project/agent configuration. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can research, react to external events and propose memory/rules, and runtime code can forecast quota exhaustion, but these paths remain current task execution, memory/governance or resource routing rather than a distinct outside-and-then adaptation function. - Evidence: packages/core/src/forecast.ts; packages/core/src/routing.ts; packages/core/src/projects.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: general-purpose agents can of course be tasked with future research, and custom workflows could be authored. Generic expressiveness does not establish a first-party S4 path under the Methodology.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: maintain durable project identity and ultimate project ground rules through an authoritative parent-owned rule surface that governs later agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: proposed changes to what the project is, how its work should be done and which durable rules/conventions future agents must follow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept or reject a proposed authoritative project-rule change (AGENTS.md) before it becomes the rule set future project sessions receive. - Decision owner: the legitimate self-hosted project operator/user in the first-party review flow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project scaffold; orientation agent; inbox; poly projects review / app Review UI; resolveInboxItem; project briefing/session context injection; agent/vendor support for AGENTS.md. - Closure path: current project/context or orientation agent surfaces a proposed rule/identity update into the project inbox → operator keeps or discards it → a kept AGENTS.md replaces the authoritative project rule file → later project sessions load/follow the changed rules. - Boundary reachability: project creation/orientation/review and per-session project briefing are shipped first-party product paths at the pinned revision; no contributor-governance or development-only actor is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents can draft/propose durable rules but are explicitly prevented from making them authoritative directly. The parent operator owns the ultimate keep/discard decision; therefore the established mode is P, not A. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the durable declaration of what the project is, how work is done and the ground rules all project agents must follow, rather than an approval of one ordinary tool call or merge. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only — the legitimate local project operator/user accepting or rejecting the proposed authoritative AGENTS.md change. - Return-to-operation path: accepted rule file becomes the project&#39;s authoritative AGENTS.md; subsequent Polyphemus sessions in that project receive/read those rules and conduct later work under them. - Evidence: docs/design/projects.md; packages/core/src/projects.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary tool approvals, merge gates and model-fallback questions are not used as S5 evidence. The positive mapping is limited to durable project identity/ground-rule governance and its returned effect on future sessions.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/669e24a89f6ea047486bf56febc9a2ed94b72904/assessments/polyphemus.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 669e24a89f6ea047486bf56febc9a2ed94b72904\">669e24a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T02:22:41+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AadiXC0DE/Rune\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rune</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AadiXC0DE/Rune/tree/538f8bdb96c926880628c102fdc2cd8c2d473fb1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 538f8bdb96c926880628c102fdc2cd8c2d473fb1\">538f8bd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: choose and perform bounded repository investigation, editing, shell/verification actions and response revision for a user&#39;s current coding task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertain repository layout, tool failures, unexpected file contents, user steering and incomplete verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the active model selects tool calls, interprets returned results and chooses the next coding step or completion; the user and fixed policy retain higher authority. - Decision owner: model actor inside the native interactive session or one-shot task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: turn::runturn, advertised tool registry, workspace context, result/event recording, budget and policy checks. - Closure path: user request → model-generated tool call → authorized tool result → history/steering feedback → next model action or final response. - Boundary reachability: rune interactive session directly calls turn::runturn with production registry; this does not depend on the dormant child bridge. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses task-level investigation and correction, whereas deterministic sandbox, limits and permission rules only bound those choices. - Evidence: turn execution loop, native session. - Basis: direct in-distribution model/tool action-and-feedback closure. - Confidence: high for code-path structure, untested task effectiveness. - Caveats: configured model service required at actual runtime; no live LLM performance measurement.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: construct bounded same-parent coordination/admission among separately identified child S1 workers, avoiding duplicate identity and exhaustion of the available child slots. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping child identities that make feedback ambiguous, multiple attempted child commitments against the same finite parent budget, and stale commands after a child has ended. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party Registry::create rejects duplicate active named children and excess children; feedback channels reject closed/full queues and only drain guidance at step boundaries, returning errors to the caller. - Decision owner: fixed first-party per-parent admission/feedback rule; a host-composed parent model would have to decide how to respond to rejection. No autonomous S2 owner is shipped in the native CLI. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ChildKind::Named, Registry, Feedback::queue/drain, Subagent::new(Delegate, Authority), authority inheritance and revocation checks. - Closure path: two potential child S1 commitments contend for the same parent name/slot → the library constructor rejects interference and exposes the failure → composed parent can redirect or wait → later eligible child request can proceed. The host must supply the actual child runner and complete decision loop. - Boundary reachability: the public rune-agent Registry/Subagent types are first-party constructor exports; live child operation requires host composition, while the standard CLI deliberately registers Unsupported and is not a positive S2 runtime witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an S2-specific admission relation exists at the constructor level, but its admission decision is deterministic; no shipped autonomous coordinator controls multiple live children. - Distinct S1 units: separate prospective registered child sessions under one parent, each with its own task brief and child identity after a host supplies Delegate. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two children with an identical addressable name would compete for the same parent messaging target, or excessive parallel child commitments would exceed the parent&#39;s declared capacity. - Attenuating coordination relation: reject duplicate names, enforce per-parent child ceiling, validate feedback queue validity and return explicit failures. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: constructor errors and bounded feedback returns let the parent change the next child request or delay it; queued steering is drained at a child boundary when supplied by a host. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: checks are scoped to competing sibling agent identities and their shared per-parent execution budget, not a generic message transport alone. - Evidence: child registry and feedback, delegate constructor, native Unsupported wiring. - Basis: function-specific constructor, not delivered autonomous CLI coordination. - Confidence: moderate; precise structural constructor, no live multi-child host proved. - Caveats: no workspace conflict resolver, scheduling policy or independent sibling agents in the standard CLI; do not upgrade this state to A on the strength of uncompiled Bridge source.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: choose and revise the current local task&#39;s overall commitments and completion order, conditional on results, within a single running session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing current task scope, diverging steps, unsuccessful verification, available token/step budget and mid-turn user correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can replace its entire live task plan (pending/inprogress/completed) and choose whether to inspect, patch, verify, retry or return a bounded partial outcome. - Decision owner: session-level coding model; user can steer individual decisions but the positive claim does not rely on a separate autonomous child hierarchy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: updateplan whole-snapshot, per-turn history/steering, one-active-step consistency check, visible state events and deterministic limits. - Closure path: task goal and current plan → model observes operational results or queued guidance → revises overall near-term plan and work priority → calls ensuing tools or returns to user. - Boundary reachability: updateplan is supplied in the native tools inventory and turn::runturn handles resulting plan events in ordinary sessions; no external controller is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting and revising current work priorities is model discretion; enforcing the maximum number of plan steps or the context budget is not itself S3 ownership. - Whole-system current view: within the deliberately narrow one-task recursion, the model sees task history, one live plan snapshot and returned tool outcomes, not a fleet-wide multi-agent portfolio. - Current-control decision scope: order/replace all current task commitments, move a different step to active status, reconsider remaining work on failure and decide completion/reporting of the local coding task. - Evidence: bounded plan replacement, turn history and steering, native tool preparation. - Basis: local-current control over the entire bounded task, not organization-wide resource allocation. - Confidence: moderate; model option present, actual fidelity not run. - Caveats: the review finds no active native child portfolio and does not credit speculative parent-wide coordination or an independent parent-mode S3 controller from mere CLI stop/steer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently challenge the S3 controller&#39;s account of live current-task management using a separate evidentiary route, then return management correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: erroneous priority/commitment accounting or uncorrected S3 narrative; no such closed complementary audit is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: absent. rune review takes a provided pending diff and invokes ordinary ask; automatic review decides whether an unresolved tool action is safe, not whether S3 is reporting management reality faithfully. - Decision owner: no in-boundary complementary S3 audit owner identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: diff capture, per-action model review/hold, ordinary user-visible session log. - Closure path: neither diff feedback nor per-action permission review is an independent S3 claim → direct evidence → corrective control path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a second review model is not S3 simply because it exists; the audited claim and corrective loop are about another function. - Evidence: one-shot diff review, action reviewer, permission routing. - Basis: bounded negative at the declared local-task management recursion. - Confidence: high for two checked reviewer paths. - Caveats: external ACP clients may independently audit S3, but those client arrangements are not shipped Rune ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: learn from outside/future distinctions and decide changes to reusable future task capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new project/tool environments, evolving methods or recurrent skill failures; no first-party prospective adaptation closure found. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established; static skills and layered instruction files are loaded, not autonomously revised from external research or operational outcomes. - Decision owner: no credited S4 owner; human maintainers can edit project/user instructions outside the assessed running harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS.md scope chain, skills catalog, prompt assembly and persisted logs/commands. - Closure path: prompt/context injection affects a present run; it is not a demonstrated future-option generation and return path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current context search, task re-planning and manually edited files do not alone imply outside-and-then intelligence. - Evidence: instructions discovery, prompt assembly, configuration. - Basis: bounded negative for first-party future adaptation. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: maintainers&#39; NEXT-PHASE.md is a roadmap, not an adaptive operating function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: set the standing owner-approved normative boundary for the local assistant&#39;s identity, provider, permissions and allowed repository operations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: attempts by untrusted repository configuration or a model action to override the user&#39;s provider/permission identity, authorization and tool-safety policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: user selects profile-owned provider, permission mode, rule settings and secrets; the project .rune.toml cannot override these keys; operator CLI/environment overrides remain explicit outside-model controls. - Decision owner: legitimate local human/host operator (P), not the task model or untrusted repository contributor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: five-layer config resolver with project-safe whitelist, PermissionMode, rule/approval routing, runtime tool-call gate and inherited child authority caps. - Closure path: operator configures durable user policy → project-owned unsafe keys are ignored with diagnostics → running host resolves and enforces effective policy on attempted actions → subsequent work stays under that operator boundary. - Boundary reachability: the native rune entry loads these settings before interactive or ask execution and applies permission mode during live tool-call decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: setting ultimate provider/permission identity belongs to the local operator; model/automatic reviewer judges an individual unresolved action within this standing policy and cannot rewrite its authority. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: whose standing safety, provider and authorization configuration may govern later local assistant sessions, including when repository contents disagree. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: user/host operator (P) alone; no agent-owned ultimate identity mode established. - Return-to-operation path: resolved Settings selects provider, working mode and permission decision path for later agent tool calls. - Evidence: config separation and precedence, project key restriction, native run settings, policy gate. - Basis: standing identity/ultimate-policy authority returning to real operation, not a per-call permission prompt alone. - Confidence: moderate-high. - Caveats: this is a local installation&#39;s policy recursion; neither OSS contributors nor model provider are a single distributed S5 parent for all installations.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3aeedb490ca2710f836de267b35e0ea765b0571c/assessments/aa/aadixc0de-rune.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3aeedb490ca2710f836de267b35e0ea765b0571c\">3aeedb4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T22:40:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/turri-oss/turri\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">turri</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/turri-oss/turri/tree/47b578cafcab669a0ed3c02b9d0f1d5e17fcd8fa\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 47b578cafcab669a0ed3c02b9d0f1d5e17fcd8fa\">47b578c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-directed coding and specification/conformance-test production deliver functional code from changing project requirements. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial or contradictory descriptions, code faults, test failures, tool results and new behavior requested by the operator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: writing/updating plain, planning a feasible implementation change and choosing edits, tests and repairs within a pulse. - Decision owner: the configured harness/planner and coding/conformance model agents in the supported turri roles; a human still owns the initiating requested purpose and may edit the source specification. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pi tool adapter, createWorkLoop, module ledgers, role-specific confined tools, test scripts, histories and retry caps. - Closure path: revised specification → planned pulse → coding-agent edits/tests → review findings and any repairs → passing scripted checks and committed module record → generated code made available to subsequent pulses/release. - Boundary reachability: turri regenerate and installed conversation enter the same first-party regeneration work loop; model-backed role executors are created for that loop, not merely present in developer examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses meaningful implementation and repair actions while turri enforces paths/test gates; static sequence rules are not themselves the agent&#39;s decisions. - Evidence: README operational flow, work-loop source, review/repair flow. - Basis: explicit supported design corroborated by first-party runtime wiring. - Confidence: high for shipped role construction; no paid-provider execution in this review. - Caveats: external pi/model providers supply inference; a no-model configured installation cannot perform this S1.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: a first-party dependency/collision lane prevents simultaneous work cells from overwriting the same module or a required upstream module. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent code/test pulses writing overlapping module directories or shared requires ancestors can race and corrupt one another&#39;s results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: map each queued pulse to its entire writable module dependency chain, withhold a conflicting pulse and retry after the occupying pulse settles. - Decision owner: a first-party deterministic registry and lane relation implements the conflict-specific path; no model is shown to choose or revise this cross-unit settlement autonomously. Developer-constructed policies define lane granularity. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: moduleLanes, job registry queue/waiting states, module dependency tracking and run ledger. - Closure path: observed overlapping lane claims → one pulse waits instead of writing → completion/release of the lane → waiting pulse may start against the updated module state. - Boundary reachability: both CLI and conversation work paths request the same first-party job lanes on the supported project files, not only a test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the S2-specific collision feedback path is shipped, but the authority and choice of this conservative coordination relation live in constructor code rather than an autonomous coordinating agent; hence C, not A. - Distinct S1 units: separately running model-backed code or conformance pulses on two modules that share a dependency or output directory. - Inter-S1 disturbance: both writing the same module chain would race and lose one worker&#39;s changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: overlapping module-directory lanes become mutually exclusive, with required ancestor chains included. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the later pulse waits and is re-planned/started after the earlier work completes and the code record is refreshed. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: moduleLanes explicitly identifies a concrete competing-write interference and ties waiting to shared writable paths, beyond simply assigning tasks in sequence. - Evidence: lane collision intent and function, work-loop wait and dependency checks, job registry. - Basis: structural code path and explicit collision rationale. - Confidence: medium-high for the conservative S2 constructor; it is not model-governed coordination. - Caveats: no negotiated or autonomous dynamic arbitration of cross-module conflicts is claimed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: the live project operator can survey current multi-module work, choose priorities/constraints, change model/concurrency configuration and halt or steer ongoing work as a whole. - Disturbance / variety regulated: competing active pulse commitments, accumulated work, cost, stalled modules and currently unwanted regeneration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the project queue/pulse status, halt an affected module, steer its pending work or alter capacity/models at the next allowed transition. - Decision owner: the legitimate human owner of the local turri project, not the panel renderer, deterministic loop or individual coding agents. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current pulse/job panel, state registry, halt, steer, and /turri-config commands with documented effective-time behavior. - Closure path: operator reads current module/pulse state → changes or halts a current commitment → work loop stops/rolls back a pulse, holds dependent modules, or uses the updated config on subsequent eligible pulses. - Boundary reachability: these commands and project-current panels are shipped in the installed conversation and CLI mode; the work loop records held/waiting modules and processes configuration changes during operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the user decides intervention and priority; deterministic suspension/enforcement carries out the choice. Autonomous agent planning within one coding job is not promoted to whole-project S3. - Whole-system current view: module-level planned/running/halted/waiting state and pulse costs/stages in the interactive panel. - Current-control decision scope: which project modules proceed now, which halt, what current compute/model/concurrency envelope applies and which pending work is steered. - Evidence: panel, halt and config supported modes, work-loop hold/wait implementation, live config commands. - Basis: explicit operator workflow plus implemented return path. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded single-project human S3. - Caveats: some settings change only on the next pulse; this is local parent current control, not upstream maintainers&#39; org-wide governance.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separately instantiated read-only reviewer independently inspects code and unit tests against the frozen specification and source diff, challenging an implementation agent&#39;s self-report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible successful code-agent narratives and green but weak tests can conceal defects or missing requirements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer names actual code/test discrepancies from direct file access; an optional judge model independently upholds or dismisses contested findings; coder must address upheld blocking findings before successful landing. - Decision owner: read-only reviewer/judge model roles supplied by the installed harness, separate from the model agent that produced code; the test runner is a deterministic gate rather than owner of their findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: exact worktree diff, specification snapshot, testing script, canitfail probes, two-stage review/arbiter and returned review notes. - Closure path: agent edits and declares work → first-party diff/raw files and test script are provided to reviewer → independent problems are returned to coder → revised code and rerun tests/review → evidence-backed landing or rollback. - Boundary reachability: the shipped implementation path invokes createUnitReviewer and arbitrateReview as role-confined executors during regeneration, with different write permissions from coding agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: critical judgment is made by independent role models inspecting code, not by a boolean status check or the coding agent&#39;s own completion statement; this is a complementary path even though run as a standard quality gate. - Claim being audited: implementation and tests actually satisfy the declared functional spec, beyond the coding agent&#39;s report and green test exit code. - Ordinary reporting path: coding agent&#39;s account, tool transcript and unit-test pass/fail result. - Complementary access path: frozen changed-file diff, direct files, test script and adversarial ability to run targeted test-failure probes. - Independence boundary: reviewer cannot write the code under review and receives an independent review task; the arbiter model rules on disputed review findings instead of letting the coder silently waive them. - Who acts on findings: the coding agent repairs issues upheld by reviewer/arbiter; deterministic harness reruns checks and records the result. - Evidence: reviewer function and read-only task, arbiter role and ruling, conformance check roles. - Basis: explicit review contract and structural read-only, diff-based model access. - Confidence: medium-high; code path inspected without invoking paid models. - Caveats: independence is role/tool/context separation, not a wholly different vendor or external human audit; findings are bounded by the specification and reviewer quality.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped distinct outside-and-then loop converting future environmental distinctions into durable organizational capability adaptation was established at the reviewed project boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing external models, frameworks, dependencies and stakeholder needs could motivate later tool/strategy changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party future-environment interpretation/option selection is shown beyond developer/operator configuration and current-spec regeneration. - Decision owner: not established for an S4 path; model selection, new requirements and project maintenance remain operator/developer choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: test advisor, model configuration, skills, plain revision, replayable regeneration and provider adapters. - Closure path: present requirements or current test failure may trigger another build, but no independently selected prospective environmental option returns to change the standing harness capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scenario exploration and internal re-planning regulate the requested product; they do not evidence continuous external/future sensing coupled to present management. - Evidence: test advisor&#39;s current-spec questions, regeneration scope, config model selection. - Basis: bounded negative first-party source review. - Confidence: medium for the supported standard modes. - Caveats: future plugins or human upgrades are possible but not credited without a shipped closure path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no shipped organizational identity/ultimate-policy deliberation, legitimate authority and return-to-operation loop was established inside one turri-controlled project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflicting long-term purpose, allowed organizational missions and ultimate authority could arise outside the provided task workflow. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator creates/edits the project&#39;s purpose and configuration; the product enforces static safety walls but does not adjudicate identity-level matters through a returning governing loop. - Decision owner: not established for a first-party S5 path; the human project owner remains ultimate authority outside this specific function claim. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: command fence, OS sandbox, file audit, model role definitions, configuration and user permissions. - Closure path: ordinary task authorization and command refusal affect execution, not a standing process for identity/policy disagreement → legitimate authority → binding new policy → changed subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a durable specification records what software to build, not who may redefine the harness organization or reconcile S3/S4 at identity level. - Evidence: safety and install overview, command-check scope, project mutation audit. - Basis: bounded negative first-party source review. - Confidence: high for no identity-level closure in shipped modes. - Caveats: contributor governance and external vendor policies are outside this declared runtime/project recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ad4dd5e7658c2cbf7c4c323fbaf8d5117a4fea0/assessments/tu/turri.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ad4dd5e7658c2cbf7c4c323fbaf8d5117a4fea0\">9ad4dd5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T21:16:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/EthanYin-0701/sangxia-ai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sangxia.ai</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/EthanYin-0701/sangxia-ai/tree/f38f484d8c7f15f6937d5da553d9c25ef1953daf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f38f484d8c7f15f6937d5da553d9c25ef1953daf\">f38f484 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: interpret a developer request, inspect/edit files, invoke tools and return a coding outcome. - Disturbance / variety regulated: variable repository structure, requested edits, tool errors, model errors, path/permission constraints and interrupted sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tool calls and arguments in response to observations and tool results; decide whether further coding iterations are needed. - Decision owner: the configured model actor in runTurn, subject to permission/timeout/hook gates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP session management, tool registry, input-schema validation, permission prompts, bash/file tools, MCP integration, streamed provider calls, bounded retries and event persistence. - Closure path: user task → model choice → validated and permitted local tool call → observable tool result in conversation history → model&#39;s next work or final answer. - Boundary reachability: standard ACP and local TUI sessions both call SangxiaAgent.prompt, which invokes the shipped model/tool loop with first-party tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic dispatch enforces calls, but the model chooses operational actions based on returned evidence. The operator can veto protected effects without owning the model&#39;s task-step choices. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/harness/loop.ts; src/tools/index.ts. - Basis: direct source tracing and offline fake-provider/ACP executable smoke tests. - Confidence: high for the shipped decision route. - Caveats: no real remote model was called; observed execution is structural/mock-backed, not measured coding success under a live provider.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no shipped inter-S1 disturbance attenuation is established inside this one-session organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible conflicting edits or resource use among independent agents, but no first-party independent peer/child agent set is operated here. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none over relations among distinct S1 units. - Decision owner: the model sequences its own tool calls; the runtime serializes concurrent prompts on the same session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: promptInFlight, tool-call ordering, ACP updates, local updateplan display, optional MCP connections. - Closure path: sequencing protects one session&#39;s history; there is no inter-S1 disturbance → coordination judgment → revised independent S1 behavior loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: plan publication and same-session serialization do not coordinate distinct autonomous operating units. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/tools/plan.ts; src/mcp/client.ts. - Basis: shipped runtime and available tool registry inspection. - Confidence: high for the declared first-party session boundary. - Caveats: an outside MCP server or multiple users could independently deploy agents, but that does not make their relations a shipped Sangxia S2 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control actor reallocating resources or commitments across a portfolio of S1 activity is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current deadlines, partial tool failures, conversation length and user-approval constraints are handled operationally, without organizational-level current steering. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no actor owns a distinct cross-operation allocation, priority intervention or accountability return for the declared recursion. - Decision owner: S1 model chooses its own steps; static caps and permission rules are configured by operator/runtime; client can cancel a session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iteration/token/timeouts, abort controller, ACP permission mode, tool plan status and hook command results. - Closure path: operational attempts continue/stop under gates; no separate whole-organization view → current-control judgment → redirected S1 commitments loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither a fixed time budget nor a generic stop/approval UI establishes a VSM S3 decision owner; user permission is per action, not portfolio governance. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/harness/loop.ts; src/harness/permissions.ts. - Basis: inspected available resource/permission/current-state decision paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: operator could supervise a real-world organization with external tooling, but no qualifying parent-governed S3 mode is closed by this runtime itself.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no separate complementary audit path checks S1 claims with a distinguishable owner and returned findings. - Disturbance / variety regulated: undetected tool errors, unsafe actions and defective edits could benefit from audit, but the shipped hooks are general user-defined instrumentation/gates. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party independent or complementary auditor decides the validity of S1 reports. - Decision owner: external user-supplied hook scripts may deny or annotate calls; Sangxia executes and reports them without supplying a separate audit role/evidence path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: pretooluse, posttooluse, lifecycle hooks, exit-code/error policies, command invocation and context injection. - Closure path: hooks may block a tool or feed diagnostics into the same model loop; there is no first-party separate claims-audit → finding → mandated correction path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a configurable program hook is a usable sensor or gate, not proof that an auditor actor, complementary access and sustained review right are shipped. - Evidence: src/hooks/index.ts; src/hooks/types.ts; src/harness/loop.ts. - Basis: inspected hook registry and tool integration. - Confidence: medium-high for stock/optional shipped configurations. - Caveats: an operator could write a reviewer as a hook, but that unobserved external composition cannot be credited as first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: inspect the project environment and autonomously synthesize durable project guidance for future coding-agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing/stale project-specific knowledge about architecture, conventions, commands and decisions that would otherwise make later coding sessions start without appropriate context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which inspected repository facts, working rules, architectural distinctions, decisions and TODOs become future project instruction/memory content. - Decision owner: the configured model actor running the dedicated project-initialization subturn; human only grants/skips initialization and may choose the scan boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: missing-file detection, explicit ACP operator permission request, scoped init prompt, read/list/glob/grep/write tool restriction, 12-iteration cap, durable AGENTS.md and .sangxia/memory.md, and systemPrompt() reload of project instructions. - Closure path: missing project instructions at the start of work → operator enables initialization → model inspects README/source/config and develops specific long-lived guidance → writes project memory files → live system prompt rebuild → later/new sessions load that guidance and change their coding context. - Boundary reachability: SangxiaAgent.prompt() invokes maybeInitializeProject before the requested work on the standard ACP/TUI route; its model-directed runTurn produces durable project files, then systemPrompt loads them from the same workspace. - External distinction: actual user project source, documentation, config, directory layout and optionally permitted parent-directory context outside the Sangxia runtime. - Future / prospective distinction: persisted instructions are explicitly designated as long-term project memory for later tasks and loaded at session start, rather than produced merely as an immediate answer. - Adaptation option generated: the model selects and writes rules/technical-stack conventions to AGENTS.md plus background/status/decisions/TODO knowledge to .sangxia/memory.md after examining the project. - Path back into current capability / S3: the initializer replaces the session system-prompt content via systemPrompt(session, initRoot) and persists it; newSession and relevant reload paths take project memory into coding-agent context, changing later operating guidance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent approves initiation and scope, but neither static initialization prompt nor file writer determines project-specific judgments. The model owns their synthesis and the files alter subsequent agent context without a second parent adoption decision. An independent parent-governed adaptation mode is not established from a simple yes/no initiation gate. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/project-memory.ts; src/tools/fs-tools.ts. - Basis: traced shipped first-use initializer, model/tool capabilities, subsequent in-memory prompt rebuild and durable read path; fake-provider smoke evidence covers neighboring runtime behavior, not real model guidance quality. - Confidence: medium-high for ownership/reachability; effect on downstream success is unmeasured. - Caveats: bounded to user-consented, first-time missing-file initialization; not evidence of continuous external scanning, self-redesign or measured strategic success. Session restoration retains its saved system message except session-start hook additions, so the strongest guaranteed later-read path is a new session; do not infer live re-scan on every resumed session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate purpose/identity policy loop governing the assistant as an organization is evidenced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: credentials, tool access and operator risk tolerance are handled as authentication or local effect permissions rather than ultimate institutional policy disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party S5 authority adjudicates competing mission/purpose/identity claims and returns binding policy changes. - Decision owner: human chooses access mode, configures provider and accepts/rejects tool and initialization prompts; maintainers own upstream source externally. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ACP authenticate, confirm/auto modes, per-tool remembered allowances, hook deny/ask behavior and system instructions. - Closure path: tactical permission and authentication gates affect a call/session, but no ultimate-policy issue → authorized identity decision → durable operational return is formed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a token check, user approval or stable system prompt is not by itself the final identity/policy governing function. - Evidence: src/agent.ts; src/harness/permissions.ts; src/config.ts. - Basis: traced authentication, configuration and policy/permission decision paths. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a larger human organization may have its own S5 outside this installed-agent boundary; repository maintainer activity is not attributed to a running session.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2dd14c4373db9f46c72ccef31e6383c5840ca102/assessments/sa/sangxia-ai.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2dd14c4373db9f46c72ccef31e6383c5840ca102\">2dd14c4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-23T19:31:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KNIGHT15235/likecc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LikeCC</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/KNIGHT15235/likecc/tree/4a9b77d281646385bf48fd85b8a03c4d28bbee23\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4a9b77d281646385bf48fd85b8a03c4d28bbee23\">4a9b77d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously implement and verify user-requested local coding tasks, including independently model-deciding child workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code state, build/test failures, shell output, incomplete task requirements, permission denial and results of delegated coding. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model chooses file/shell operations and child task instructions, then selects subsequent actions on observed results. - Decision owner: LikeCC primary model and separately instantiated child/teammate model agents within their work scopes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider, conversation loop, tool registry, permission checker, AgentTool, TaskManager, team/worktree setup and notifications. - Closure path: request → model tool/agent decision → checked native execution → result, error or child completion fed into a later model turn → revised action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: interactive app.py and headless main.py both instantiate the runtime plus the first-party AgentTool and Team system; separately enabled workers run through real model loops. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes and restricts commands, but the substantive task choice and response to tool evidence are model decisions. - Evidence: agent.py, agenttool.py, main.py. - Basis: pinned first-party source and native script/unit tests; no provider-connected runs. - Confidence: high for installation and control wiring. - Caveats: passing deterministic tests does not establish LLM coding efficacy or quality of model decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no complete first-party disturbance-specific inter-S1 coordination and correction circuit is established at the team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: distinct coding teammates may propose incompatible edits, overlap shared task dependencies or require conflict resolution at integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no modeled, observable agent-owned assessment of an actual incompatible peer change followed by a conflict-resolving inter-S1 direction is established. - Decision owner: each worker selects its own coding tasks; team worktree construction is automatic and current lead dispatch is task routing rather than proved interference-specific arbitration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: mandatory teammate-specific Git worktrees, shared task blocking fields, team mailbox and explicit coordinator prompt advice to serialize file-overlapping work. - Closure path: isolated teammate work and returned status exist, but there is no evidenced peer conflict/incompatibility detection → model-selected attenuation or integration decision → changed S1 execution based on that assessment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: per-worker isolation and generic assignments prevent some direct overwrites; their creation is predetermined machinery rather than an agent deciding how to resolve detected inter-worker interference and closing that decision. - Evidence: agenttool.py, sharedtask.py, coordinator.py. - Basis: pinned source-level evaluation against five-witness S2 calibration. - Confidence: medium-high; conservative classification. - Caveats: a supported custom workflow could request a conflict review, but not enough is installed as S2-specific standard ownership here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: a dedicated first-party coordinator-mode constructor targets present whole-team work and priorities, but the full autonomous intervention loop is not wired. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneously executing workers, changed readiness, task failures, unknown current worker progress and conflicting commitments within one local coding team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinator model can synthesize results, issue initial/nested Agent assignments and choose next work from returned task notifications; the advertised follow-up/stop and whole-task inspection tools are missing from its live registry. - Decision owner: configured team-lead model selects initial and follow-on work, but the decisive whole-current feedback/reassignment right is only partially constructed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: two-flag coordinator opt-in, first-party role prompt, TeamCreateTool, AgentTool, filtered lead registry, persisted TeamManager and child completion notifications. - Closure path: enabled lead → TeamCreate switches role → model delegates workers → runtime delivers completion or mailbox notifications → model may create new work; missing SendMessage/TaskStop registration and lead-side TaskList prevent the claimed direct current-worker correction/readback path. - Boundary reachability: optional configuration and environment opt-in activate the installed role when a team is created in the interactive runtime; headless likewise constructs TeamCreateTool, while all missing-tool limitations persist. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: coordinator-specific model discretion and role are purpose-built and first-party (hence C rather than generic delegation), but role text naming a tool cannot establish that tool&#39;s actual decision/feedback authority. - Whole-system current view: model sees its delegated task history and returned notifications but no registered live team-wide TaskList/member-status query; this incompleteness limits whole-current control. - Current-control decision scope: initial decomposition, dispatch and future new worker instructions are reachable; targeted follow-up, stop or reassignment of an active teammate is not fully available to the coordinator lead. - Evidence: coordinator.py, toolfilter.py, teamcreate.py, app.py, main.py. - Basis: positive function-specific optional constructor, with missing registration verified through both entry points and default registry. - Confidence: medium-high for constructor and negative actual-tool wiring conclusion. - Caveats: C does not credit closed autonomous current control. A future code change registering functional lead-side status/message/stop tools would require new pinned evidence and reassessment.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: in explicitly configured first-party Verification mode, independently challenge another worker&#39;s implementation/verification claim by inspecting actual project artifacts and test commands. - Disturbance / variety regulated: premature completion claims, self-serving implementation reports, missed regressions, unexecuted builds/tests and bugs hidden by producer assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a fresh specialized Verification model independently selects evidence checks and issues VERDICT: PASS / FAIL / PARTIAL; configured lead may use failed evidence to assign a new correcting worker. - Decision owner: different worker model with a new conversation and verification-specific instructions, not the implementation worker&#39;s own summary or test string. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bundled verification.md, opt-in AgentLoader, per-agent filtered tool registry denying named file-edit tools, AgentTool background completion, first-party coordinator&#39;s fresh-verifier and error-follow-up policy. - Closure path: producing worker performs changes and claims completion → coordinator spawns separate freshly instructed Verification worker with access to actual project artifacts → verifier runs build/test/lint, independently challenges claim and returns explicit verdict via task notification → coordinator can assign correction or withhold verified completion. - Boundary reachability: set enableverificationagent: true so standard loader exposes the shipped named role; enable coordinator with config plus environment and create a team to install the mandate. For same-root ordinary subagents, the verifier can inspect the edited workspace; worktree-isolated teammates require explicit artifact/branch access or integration before the audit can see their edits. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier, not a fixed CI assertion, makes a claim-relative judgment after independent artifact access; correction selection remains with the coordinating model. It is not an automatic runtime-enforced pass/fail gate. - Claim being audited: the implementation worker&#39;s assertion that the requested code change is complete, builds correctly and introduces no unreported regression. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer&#39;s own completion text, tool output and task-notification to the lead. - Complementary access path: fresh verifier reads implementation/configuration from disk and runs independent build, tests, type/lint and regression checks using its own tool calls, then reports actual commands/output and verdict. - Independence boundary: distinct fresh worker context and artifact access, restricted named edit tools; shared parent provider, potential shell side effects and absence of a runtime mandatory audit gate limit procedural independence. - Who acts on findings: lead model may launch a correcting worker or report incomplete verification; no downstream merge veto is mechanically guaranteed. - Evidence: verification.md, loader.py, agenttool.py, coordinator.py. - Basis: first-party shipped opt-in operating path, built-in specialist definition, native role/configuration and coordinator tests; no live verifier was run. - Confidence: medium; supported role/return path confirmed, actual quality and model compliance unknown. - Caveats: default setup leaves Verification disabled, the model must obey its prompt, and the independent worker must reach the actual modified artifact rather than an untouched sibling worktree. This is supported option capability, not proof that every LikeCC task was independently audited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed outside/future environmental sensing and selected, retained organizational capability change is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing requirements and historical context affect coding sessions, but no future-oriented capability mismatch/adaptation loop is installed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can plan immediate tasks and user can configure roles/providers; no S4 owner decides durable capability redesign using an external future horizon. - Decision owner: absent for S4; task execution and plugin configuration belong to other recursions/functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project instructions, memory/session compaction, configurable prompts, user-defined agents, model configuration and Git history. - Closure path: retrieved context can alter current coding instructions, not a horizon scan → prospective option → adopted reusable capability → returned current-system support. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: implementing software for a user&#39;s present task is not necessarily strategic adaptation of the harness&#39;s own capabilities. - Evidence: prompts.py, config.py, agent.py. - Basis: local first-party source and runtime modes. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: downstream agent-authored plugins could be developed, but generic ability to write code does not itself establish the organizational S4 circuit.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity-defining legitimate policy arbitrator mediating present vs future organizational choices is installed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dangerous tool effects and user permission boundaries are immediate operational constraints, not ultimate identity/purpose disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator/config sets policy and runtime enforces per-tool constraints; no S5 organization-wide ultimate-policy decision actor is specified. - Decision owner: no first-party S5 actor identified in the declared run-time recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionChecker, sandbox rules, bounded child permission inheritance, team role scope and configuration. - Closure path: immediate permission allow/deny returns to tool execution; no identity-level dispute → legitimate authority choice → durable organization-wide governing return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an operator&#39;s execution consent or static rule does not by itself constitute organizational purpose/identity governance. - Evidence: permissions/checker.py, main.py, config.py. - Basis: core permissions/configuration and local-vs-OSS boundary. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: legitimate human ownership of a real OSS organization is outside the analyzed local harness runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/405f47ac27e8faaed531d00c91f6cfff3b3c66d9/assessments/li/likecc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 405f47ac27e8faaed531d00c91f6cfff3b3c66d9\">405f47a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T15:15:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gabyic/agentdock-mcp-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentDock MCP Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gabyic/agentdock-mcp-harness/tree/d5f06ba7c712be032ff7788175cb04d322bead7b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d5f06ba7c712be032ff7788175cb04d322bead7b\">d5f06ba &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete user-requested remote code investigation, editing, test and commit work via the explicitly supported chat agent/MCP service combination. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code/task conditions, failing tests, external command output and bugs requiring choices that cannot be predetermined by a tool schema. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the connected chat model interprets files and test feedback, chooses which edit or command to perform, decides whether to retry, and invokes task completion with evidence. - Decision owner: the external MCP-capable chat agent in the documented AgentDock operating mode; first-party AgentDock tools execute and persist its selected actions without a server-side second model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task.create, file/read/patch tools, process.start, Git worktree/commit, approval/policy gates, persisted Task state and audit trail. - Closure path: user coding objective → chat agent inspects remote repository through MCP → agent chooses source edits/tests → AgentDock executes and returns actual results → same agent revises or completes the Task. - Boundary reachability: README documents live ChatGPT Web end-to-end acceptance and ships an installable MCP endpoint with executable file/process/git/task tools for that exact use; the external chat model is a required explicitly supported runtime component, not an unrelated adjacent coding product credited by assumption. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the server&#39;s process/tool implementations are deterministic and cannot decide the meaning of a failing test or appropriate fix; the integrated chat agent makes that discretionary operational choice. - Evidence: README execution loop and boundary, registered MCP tools, task worktree state. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this positive claim depends on the first-party documented composite chat-agent mode. AgentDock&#39;s server in isolation has no reasoning agent, nor does the package guarantee any specific ChatGPT/Claude subscription quota.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no separately established coordination decision/feedback loop among distinct simultaneously operating S1 agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple tasks may touch the same Git source and processes may have competing leases, but the implementation separates worktree state and reconciles ownership deterministically. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no specific autonomous inter-agent coordination right found; worktree allocation and owner leases enforce fixed rules. - Decision owner: Git/worktree, task/lease and process reconciliation machinery; ordinary chat-agent task planning and phase changes belong to S3, not S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: isolated Git worktrees per write-capable Task, persistent task IDs, process execution-owner leases, idempotency keys and task reconciliation. - Closure path: separate task worktrees and lease checks prevent cross-write contamination or false resumption; no distinct coordination judgment is returned to change another S1 agent&#39;s behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: concurrency-safe storage and isolation protect operations, but the shipped mode does not provide a first-party multi-agent cross-S1 regulator. - Evidence: README worktree and crash handling, task service, process reconciliation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: one connected chat client may use external subagent features, but such unbundled features are not AgentDock-owned S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: control current whole coding-workflow commitments, route the active software project through explicit phases, and decide whether to continue, correct, or finish the task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: vague user goals, unresolved product decisions, unfinished implementation, failed verification and interrupted sessions can cause inappropriate transitions or premature completion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the integrated chat agent classifies the work, chooses the next step/skill from current Task and workflow status, records open decisions, proposes or revises bounded work plans, and invokes permitted transitions after substantive acceptance. The server refuses invalid transitions and protects human decision boundaries. - Decision owner: the connected chat agent for task-specific current work choice; user retains original requirements and necessary product/approval decisions, while the finite-state engine only enforces allowed transitions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow.start/status/guide/update/advance, durable state, recommended skills, plan steps and explicit reasoning/human barriers, Task completion contracts and approval records. - Closure path: persisted whole-task goal/phase and unresolved decisions → chat agent interprets current project condition and chooses next commitment → AgentDock records phase/plan/task action → the next work iteration uses the updated state or stops at an unresolved human boundary. - Boundary reachability: the shipped MCP server exposes these workflow and Plan endpoints and documents their normal use by a chat agent responding to &#39;continue this project&#39;; no server-side planner is presumed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the server can suggest a skill and reject an illegal phase jump, but the agent makes the contextual choice about the phase&#39;s actual completion, suitable task actions and evidence needed for acceptance. - Whole-system current view: one repository&#39;s durable project goal, phase, open decisions, artifact paths, recommended next skill, task/worktree status and recent tool outputs. - Current-control decision scope: current project phase, task commitments, action order, next required evidence and final task completion within this user-authorized software job. - Evidence: workflow guidance and explicit transitions, workflow implementation, plan barriers. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: there is a single integrated chat reasoning actor; this is a task/workflow S3 role, not a claim that the AgentDock server independently manages multiple agent workers. Generic human approval is insufficient to add (P) at this boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party independent audit of the chat agent&#39;s whole-task control and claims using complementary operational access. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a wrong edit or premature completion could pass the same agent&#39;s review; some evidence is independently recorded but audit judgment is not delegated to an independent actor by the core distribution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped separate auditor with an independent model judgment and corrective return to S3 is wired; test exit codes, process logs and user audit records are factual checks. - Decision owner: same chat agent and optional human reviewer; any Standards/Spec multi-pass review is instruction from separately installed upstream skills, not a bundled independent audit mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured event audit, Git diffs, test completion evidence, workflow review phase and third-party code-review skill compatibility. - Closure path: operational records → original model/user inspection; no first-party independent complementary-audit verdict cycle is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic verification and two sequential passes in one context do not by themselves create an independent S3 actor. - Evidence: audit and checks, upstream skill dependency and missing host subagents, audit service. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a connected host might independently supply specialized reviewers, but they are not guaranteed or first-party packaged here.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced prospective sensing/adaptation of the AgentDock work-cell&#39;s future operating capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future task environments or installed skills may change, but the service&#39;s guidance and skill updates are operator-initiated tools rather than an S4 intelligence loop. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none beyond user-authorized current software planning and explicit installed skill updates. - Decision owner: user for installing/updating skill sources and software releases; external chat agent chooses present coding tasks and workflow steps. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git-backed skill install/update, persistent workflow phases, memory of artifacts and status, repository plans. - Closure path: no first-party outside/then sensing → future adaptation option → capability adoption/feedback loop established for this task cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning future product code is an S1/S3 coding job, not automatically proactive adaptation of the harness&#39;s own capabilities. - Evidence: skill/update and workflow scope, managed upgrade explicitly not automatic. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an installed third-party skill could teach a chat model an adaptation process; that unpinned external logic is not bundled as AgentDock S4.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no in-boundary identity/ultimate-policy adjudication path for the AgentDock task system. - Disturbance / variety regulated: host safety, approvals, task scopes and human product decisions are important constraints but do not constitute identity-level organizational tension. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no autonomous or legitimate in-boundary parent S5 loop identified; the human sets policy rules and approves/denies sensitive individual actions. - Decision owner: user/OS administration outside the coding task; deterministic policy service evaluates configured allow/ask/deny rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: approval requests, per-task scopes, secret-redacted audit, host/workspace policy, plan human barrier. - Closure path: configured policy → evaluation and human action approval → tool execution/denial; no identity-level issue escalated to ultimate authority and returned as a governing policy decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: permission grants and a HUMANBARRIER are operational authorization, not S5 purpose/identity adjudication. - Evidence: policy implementation, approval service, Plan barriers. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the host organization may have its own policies, but they are not an assessed AgentDock task-cell S5 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b5674c3f4b22f84f49006e68ed608ee86e02691d/assessments/ag/agentdock-mcp-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b5674c3f4b22f84f49006e68ed608ee86e02691d\">b5674c3 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
